NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both TanStack AI and Vercel AI SDK are open-source TypeScript toolkits for building AI-powered applications. They share common ground - streaming chat, tool calling, multi-provider support, and deploy-anywhere flexibility - but they approach the problem from fundamentally different directions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI treats AI as a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"library composition problem"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Every piece - adapters, tools, agent loops, transport, UI - is a composable building block. You import what you need, compose it how you want, and ship it wherever you want. No platform layer, no gateway abstraction, no implicit associations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK treats AI as a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"full-stack platform problem"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It provides a broad surface area of primitives with optional platform integration for gateway routing, observability, and deployment optimization."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This article compares the two SDKs from TanStack AI's perspective, with honest acknowledgment of where each excels."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"feature-comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature Comparison"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Versions referenced below: TanStack AI as of this writing; Vercel AI SDK "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ai@7.x"},{"type":"text","value":" (v7 is current)."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"License"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MIT"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apache 2.0"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hosting"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Works anywhere"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Works anywhere"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"16 official LLM adapters (OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Vertex, Grok, Groq, OpenRouter, Ollama, Bedrock, BytePlus, Mistral, Cohere, ElevenLabs, fal, Vercel Gateway, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"openaiCompatible"},{"type":"text","value":") plus 5 harness adapters; community adapters for more"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"~38 first-party provider packages (plus community); 100+ models via AI Gateway"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Framework Hooks"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React, Solid, Svelte, Vue, Preact, Angular (+ React Native)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React, Vue, Svelte, Angular (Solid is community-maintained)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation UI Hooks"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One hook per activity: chat, structured output, image, audio, speech, transcription, summarize, video, realtime"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useCompletion"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useObject"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wire Protocol"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native AG-UI events end to end"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Proprietary UI Message Stream; AG-UI via external translation layer"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in with configurable chunk strategies"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in with progressive delivery"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chat Persistence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" middleware + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":"; server store or browser stores ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localStorage"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sessionStorage"},{"type":"text","value":", IndexedDB)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guide: you own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveChat"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadChat"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validateUIMessages"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation Persistence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" restores long media runs into the same hook fields"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"-"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":" in core; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" reconnects on its own"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Redis + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumable-stream"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activeStreamId"},{"type":"text","value":"; you wire POST and GET"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Sandbox Runs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Detach, journal, takeover, reaper; the agent keeps working after the tab closes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HarnessAgent"},{"type":"text","value":" + Workflow SDK (experimental); centered on Vercel Sandbox"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Calling"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isomorphic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server()"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" system"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool()"},{"type":"text","value":" objects; client execution via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onToolCall"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Agent Loop Control"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable strategy functions "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(state) => boolean"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopWhen"},{"type":"text","value":" conditions + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Agent"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolLoopAgent"},{"type":"text","value":") class"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Approval"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-tool "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" with batched approval flow"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-tool "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"needsApproval"},{"type":"text","value":" (human-in-the-loop)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Safety"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-model type narrowing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-provider types"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tree-Shaking"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate adapter per activity (text, image, speech, etc.)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Monolithic provider packages"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy Tool Discovery"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in - works across every provider"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anthropic-only tool search with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deferLoading"},{"type":"text","value":" (provider-hosted)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Connection Adapters"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SSE, HTTP stream, XHR (SSE/stream), RPC, direct async iterables, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"},{"type":"text","value":", custom"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SSE-based data stream protocol ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatTransport"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"App-level lifecycle hooks (config, iterations, chunks, tool calls, usage, errors)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model-level wrapping via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wrapLanguageModel()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extend Adapter"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add custom/fine-tuned models with per-model type narrowing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"customProvider()"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createProviderRegistry()"},{"type":"text","value":" (string model ids)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Structured Outputs"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputPart"},{"type":"text","value":", streamed alongside tools and preserved per turn in message history"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateObject()"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"streamObject()"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Output"},{"type":"text","value":" API (per-call; no structured-output message part)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Image Generation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable API with per-model type safety (OpenAI, Gemini, Grok, OpenRouter, fal.ai)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateImage()"},{"type":"text","value":" (stable)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Video Generation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable API with async job lifecycle (OpenAI, fal.ai)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_generateVideo()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text-to-Speech"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable API, 6 output formats, speed control (OpenAI, Gemini, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateSpeech()"},{"type":"text","value":" (experimental)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transcription"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable API with word timestamps and diarization (OpenAI, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transcribe()"},{"type":"text","value":" (experimental)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Audio / Music Generation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateAudio()"},{"type":"text","value":" for music & sound effects (Gemini, ElevenLabs, fal.ai)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"-"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Summarization"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dedicated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"summarize()"},{"type":"text","value":" with streaming and style options"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"-"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5 isolate drivers you run yourself: Node "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isolated-vm"},{"type":"text","value":", QuickJS WASM, QuickJS Bun, Cloudflare, Daytona"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Experimental "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ai-sdk/code-mode"},{"type":"text","value":" (QuickJS only, Node 22+, not browser or edge); also provider-hosted code-execution tools"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode Skills"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"LLM-writable persistent skill library"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static skill uploads; no model-built library"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coding Agent Sandboxes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First-party Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode + any ACP agent via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acpCompatible"},{"type":"text","value":"; local-process, Docker, Docker Sandboxes ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":"), Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Cloudflare"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HarnessAgent"},{"type":"text","value":" (experimental): Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, Deep Agents, Pi; centered on Vercel Sandbox"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Realtime Voice"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable: OpenAI, Grok, and ElevenLabs with VAD modes and tool support"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Experimental "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_useRealtime"},{"type":"text","value":" (OpenAI, Google, xAI + Gateway)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DevTools"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isomorphic in-app panel via TanStack DevTools (all frameworks, media previews)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"devToolsMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" + local inspector (server-side, dev-only)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debug Logging"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One flag, per-category toggles, pluggable logger"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Warning logs + experimental telemetry hooks"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Client"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Standalone host-side client ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-mcp"},{"type":"text","value":") + provider-routed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcpTool()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ai-sdk/mcp"},{"type":"text","value":", stable)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Apps (Interactive Widgets)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ui://"},{"type":"text","value":" widgets via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@mcp-ui/client"},{"type":"text","value":"; React + Preact + framework-agnostic bridge; multi-server routing, pluggable session store, link-scheme hardening"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_MCPAppRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" (React only); model-vs-app tool split; iframe sandbox + allowlist"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Platform Association"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None - pure library"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional Vercel integration"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-tanstack-ai-excels","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where TanStack AI Excels"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"per-model-type-safety","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-Model Type Safety"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you select a provider and model, TypeScript narrows the exact options, capabilities, and input modalities available for that specific model - not a union of everything the provider supports."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each provider adapter contains a comprehensive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"model-meta.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" that maps every model to its capabilities: supported input modalities, context windows, and provider-specific options. When you write "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"openaiText('gpt-5.5')"},{"type":"text","value":", the type system knows exactly what that model can do."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\n// TypeScript knows gpt-5.5 supports text + image input\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{\n    role: 'user',\n    content: [\n      { type: 'text', content: 'What is in this image?' },\n      { type: 'image', source: { type: 'url', value: 'https://example.com/photo.jpg' } },\n    ],\n  }],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you pass an image content part to a text-only model, TypeScript catches it at compile time."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tree-shakeable-adapters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tree-Shakeable Adapters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every AI activity - chat, summarization, image generation, speech, transcription, video - is a separate import. Every provider exposes separate adapter functions per activity. If your app only uses chat, image generation code never enters your bundle."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Only chat code is bundled - nothing else\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\n// vs. importing activities you actually need\nimport { chat, generateImage } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText, openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is architectural, not incidental. Each adapter implements a specific interface ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TextAdapter"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ImageAdapter"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TTSAdapter"},{"type":"text","value":", etc.) and lives in its own module. Modern bundlers eliminate everything you don't import."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"isomorphic-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isomorphic Tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition()"},{"type":"text","value":" creates a shared contract - name, description, input schema, output schema - that can be implemented for different runtimes. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server()"},{"type":"text","value":" adds a server-side implementation with access to databases and APIs. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" adds a client-side implementation that runs in the browser."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\n// Define once - shared validation contract\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'addToCart',\n  description: 'Add an item to the shopping cart',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n})\n\n// Server implementation - database access\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async ({ itemId, quantity }) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.addItem(itemId, quantity)\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id }\n})\n\n// Client implementation - runs in the browser\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client(async ({ itemId, quantity }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/cart`, {\n    method: 'POST',\n    body: JSON.stringify({ itemId, quantity }),\n  })\n  return res.json()\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same schema validates inputs and outputs on both sides. The type system tracks whether a tool is a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ServerTool"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ClientTool"},{"type":"text","value":" at compile time."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK defines tools with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool()"},{"type":"text","value":" helper and does support client-side execution - a tool with no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute"},{"type":"text","value":" function is handled in the browser via the UI hook's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onToolCall"},{"type":"text","value":" callback, with the result returned through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addToolOutput"},{"type":"text","value":" (renamed from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addToolResult"},{"type":"text","value":" in v6). What it doesn't have is a single shared contract that produces separate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" implementations: server and client tool code are declared independently rather than derived from one definition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"composable-agent-loop-strategies","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable Agent Loop Strategies"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI provides agent loop control as composable pure functions. Each strategy is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(state) => boolean"},{"type":"text","value":" - return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" to continue, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" to stop."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, maxIterations, untilFinishReason, combineStrategies } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Help me plan a trip.' }]\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools,\n  agentLoopStrategy: combineStrategies([\n    maxIterations(10),\n    untilFinishReason(['stop', 'length']),\n  ]),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"combineStrategies"},{"type":"text","value":" composes them with AND logic - all strategies must agree to continue. You can add custom strategies alongside built-in ones:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { maxIterations, untilFinishReason, combineStrategies } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { estimatedCost, budget } from './cost'\n\ncombineStrategies([\n  maxIterations(10),\n  untilFinishReason(['stop']),\n  // Custom: stop if budget exceeded\n  ({ iterationCount }) => estimatedCost(iterationCount) < budget,\n])","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK (v5+) controls agent loops via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopWhen"},{"type":"text","value":", which accepts composable stopping conditions like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stepCountIs(n)"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasToolCall(name)"},{"type":"text","value":" (the default is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stepCountIs(20)"},{"type":"text","value":"), and v6 adds a dedicated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Agent"},{"type":"text","value":" abstraction (the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolLoopAgent"},{"type":"text","value":" class) that bundles model, tools, instructions, and loop settings into a reusable object. The remaining difference is in the composition model: TanStack AI's strategies are arbitrary "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(state) => boolean"},{"type":"text","value":" predicates you write inline and combine with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"combineStrategies"},{"type":"text","value":", so a stopping condition can encode any business logic (token budgets, cost ceilings, custom state checks) without waiting for a built-in condition to exist. Vercel's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopWhen"},{"type":"text","value":" also accepts custom functions, so the gap here is smaller than it once was."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"lazy-tool-discovery","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy Tool Discovery"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When your application has dozens of tools, sending all their schemas to the LLM on every request wastes tokens. TanStack AI solves this with lazy tool discovery."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark tools as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazy: true"},{"type":"text","value":" and they won't be sent to the LLM initially. Instead, a synthetic discovery tool is injected that lets the LLM request tool schemas on demand:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst searchProducts = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'searchProducts',\n  description: 'Search the product catalog',\n  lazy: true, // Not sent to LLM initially\n  inputSchema: z.object({ query: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ id: z.string(), name: z.string() })),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The LLM sees a lightweight "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"__lazy__tool__discovery__"},{"type":"text","value":" tool listing available tool names. When it needs one, it calls the discovery tool to get the full schema, then uses the real tool. For applications with large tool inventories, this significantly reduces per-request token costs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK 6 added a provider-specific analogue for Anthropic: the tool search provider tool ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolSearchBm25"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolSearchRegex"},{"type":"text","value":") with per-tool "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deferLoading"},{"type":"text","value":", where deferred tools are excluded from the initial prompt and discovered on demand. It's Anthropic-only and runs provider-side; TanStack AI's lazy discovery works across every provider and runs in your own agent loop."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"model-context-protocol-mcp","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI connects to MCP servers two ways, and you can mix them in a single "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" run:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Host-side client"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-mcp"},{"type":"text","value":") - your server connects directly to any MCP server. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" (single server) and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClients"},{"type":"text","value":" (multi-server pool) discover and execute tools, read resources, and fetch prompts over Streamable HTTP, SSE, or stdio transports, with OAuth 2.1 ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"authProvider"},{"type":"text","value":") and static-token auth."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider-routed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcpTool()"},{"type":"text","value":") - the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"provider"}]},{"type":"text","value":" connects to the MCP server on your behalf (OpenAI Responses API, Anthropic), so no MCP traffic flows through your server at all."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host-side client goes beyond basic discovery:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managed lifecycle"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - hand clients to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" option and it discovers tools and closes connections when the run ends - no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"try/finally"},{"type":"text","value":" per route."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-server pools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClients"},{"type":"text","value":" connects to many servers in parallel, auto-prefixing each server's tools to prevent name collisions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Three modes of type safety"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - untyped auto-discovery, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition()"},{"type":"text","value":"-typed allowlists with Zod validation, or fully generated per-server types via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tanstack-ai-mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" CLI."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lazy discovery"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tools({ lazy: true })"},{"type":"text","value":" defers sending tool schemas to the LLM, plugging into TanStack AI's lazy tool discovery to cut token usage on tool-heavy servers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resources & prompts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - inject MCP resources and prompts into a run with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcpResourceToContentPart"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcpPromptToMessages"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'What tools are available?' }]\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\n\n// chat() discovers the tools and closes the client when the run ends\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: { clients: [mcp] },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":" tries protocol "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2026-07-28"},{"type":"text","value":" first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A client app imports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@modelcontextprotocol/client"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A server app imports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@modelcontextprotocol/server"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package names for an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk"},{"type":"text","value":" import are in "},{"type":"link","href":"../migration/mcp-sdk","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP SDK packages"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ai-sdk/mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClient"},{"type":"text","value":") is a stable host-side client with HTTP/SSE transports, OAuth, resource reading, and prompt templates. TanStack AI's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-mcp"},{"type":"text","value":" matches that surface and adds generated end-to-end types, multi-server pools, lazy discovery, a managed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" lifecycle, and the provider-routed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcpTool()"},{"type":"text","value":" alternative."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"mcp-apps-interactive-widgets","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Apps (Interactive Widgets)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both SDKs implement "},{"type":"link","href":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Apps"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — the ratified MCP extension (standardized 2026-01-26) where a server returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ui://"},{"type":"text","value":" resource so a tool result renders as an interactive widget in a sandboxed iframe instead of raw JSON. Both keep the widget HTML out of model input and render it in a sandboxed iframe with a tool allowlist and safe link handling. TanStack AI's implementation is more built out along three axes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"More than one framework."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Widgets render via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react/mcp-apps"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-preact/mcp-apps"},{"type":"text","value":", and the bridge that routes widget actions ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMcpAppBridge"},{"type":"text","value":") lives in the framework-agnostic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-client"},{"type":"text","value":", so a new framework only needs a thin renderer. Vercel's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_MCPAppRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" and its bridge live in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ai-sdk/react"},{"type":"text","value":" — React only."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-server routing."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UIResourcePart"},{"type":"text","value":" carries a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"serverId"},{"type":"text","value":" (the pool prefix from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMCPClients"},{"type":"text","value":"), and interactive calls route back to the exact server that produced the widget — automatically when you run a multi-server pool. The call handler also enforces an unconditional same-server exposure check ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolName"},{"type":"text","value":" must be a tool that server actually exposes) with an optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowTool"},{"type":"text","value":" restriction AND-ed on top."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Session persistence & serverless-safety."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The call handler reconnects per call from a transport descriptor (stateless, serverless-safe by default), and stateful transports opt into a pluggable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"McpSessionStore"},{"type":"text","value":" — an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inMemoryMcpSessionStore"},{"type":"text","value":" ships, and SQL/KV backends drop in behind the same interface."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, useMcpAppBridge } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { MCPAppResource } from '@tanstack/ai-react/mcp-apps'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  // Routes widget tool-calls to /api/mcp-apps/call by serverId; only http/https/mailto links pass through.\n  const bridge = useMcpAppBridge({\n    threadId: 'weather-chat',\n    callEndpoint: '/api/mcp-apps/call',\n    chat: { sendMessage: async (content) => void sendMessage({ content }) },\n    onLink: (url) => window.open(url, '_blank', 'noopener'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {messages.map((m) =>\n        m.parts.map((part, i) =>\n          part.type === 'ui-resource' ? (\n            <MCPAppResource\n              key={i}\n              part={part}\n              bridge={bridge}\n              sandbox={{ url: new URL('https://your-app.example.com/mcp-sandbox.html') }}\n            />\n          ) : null,\n        ),\n      )}\n    </>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK 7 covers the core flow and adds one thing TanStack AI doesn't: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"splitMCPAppTools"},{"type":"text","value":", which separates "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"model-visible"}]},{"type":"text","value":" tools from "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"app-only"}]},{"type":"text","value":" tools the widget can call but the model never sees. Both implementations are new — Vercel marks its renderer "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_"},{"type":"text","value":", and TanStack AI's writeback of widget tool-calls into chat history is still out of scope. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../mcp/apps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP Apps guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full API."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"headless-client-architecture","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Headless Client Architecture"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" is a framework-agnostic class that manages the entire chat lifecycle - streaming, message state, tool execution, approval flows, and connection management. Every framework integration wraps this single client:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react"},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" hook wraps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-solid"},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" hook wraps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-vue"},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" composable wraps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-svelte"},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChat"},{"type":"text","value":" wraps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" (Svelte 5 runes)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-preact"},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" hook wraps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-angular"},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"injectChat"},{"type":"text","value":" wraps "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No framework-specific logic in the core. If a new framework emerges, it only needs a thin reactive wrapper."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" also accepts a persistence adapter ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClientPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":") for saving and restoring conversations client-side, and a typed runtime "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context"},{"type":"text","value":" that flows through to tools and middleware."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A reload or a second device must not wipe the conversation. TanStack AI treats that as a first-class layer, not a guide you implement by hand."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" writes the transcript, run status, and pending approvals into your store. The client has two modes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence: true"}]},{"type":"text","value":" puts the server in charge. The browser caches nothing and asks the server for the thread on mount."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence: <adapter>"}]},{"type":"text","value":" puts the browser in charge, with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localStoragePersistence()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sessionStoragePersistence()"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"indexedDBPersistence()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nfunction Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'support-chat',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n  return <button onClick={() => sendMessage('hi')}>{messages.length}</button>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence: true"},{"type":"text","value":", add a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" that calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":". That handler returns the stored transcript. If you also pass a durability adapter, the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" can resume a run that is still streaming. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK documents message persistence as a pattern. You write "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveChat"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadChat"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validateUIMessages"},{"type":"text","value":". There is no middleware that owns the transcript for you."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Long media runs (video, batch images) use a second middleware, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":". A reload restores "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" into the same generation hook. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../persistence/generation-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation Persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"resumable-streams","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A dropped socket is a different problem from a missing transcript. The model already ran. You want the rest of the tokens, not a second bill."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a durability adapter to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":". The adapter records every chunk before delivery. On reconnect the client sends the last offset and the server replays the log."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the log in process memory. Use it in development."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-durable-stream"},{"type":"text","value":" writes to an external "},{"type":"link","href":"https://durablestreams.com","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":" backend. Use it in production."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" reconnects on its own. You add a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" that calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n  })\n}\n\nexport function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In production, swap "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream(request)"},{"type":"text","value":" for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream(request, options)"},{"type":"text","value":". Everything else stays the same. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK resume needs Redis, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumable-stream"},{"type":"text","value":" package, an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activeStreamId"},{"type":"text","value":" you store, and two routes you write. The SDK gives "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resume"},{"type":"text","value":" on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"consumeSseStream"},{"type":"text","value":" callback. The store and the wiring stay yours."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"durable-sandbox-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Sandbox Runs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A coding agent can run for ten minutes. The tab will close. The wifi will drop. The next request can land on a different host."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without durability, TanStack AI destroys the sandbox on disconnect. That stops a runaway bill. With durability, the agent detaches, writes a journal at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/tmp/tanstack-runs/<runId>.ndjson"},{"type":"text","value":", and a later "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" can take the run over."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Turn it on with both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durability"},{"type":"text","value":" on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":". Passing only one leaves the default destroy-on-disconnect behavior."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const adapter = memoryStream(request)\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        runs: persistence.stores.runs,\n        durability: { adapter },\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, { durability: { adapter } })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schedule the reaper, or sandboxes keep billing after no reader is left. Use a distributed lock across hosts. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/durable-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK runs long harness work through experimental "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HarnessAgent"},{"type":"text","value":" and the Workflow SDK, centered on Vercel Sandbox. TanStack AI uses the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" path on every provider: local process, Docker, Docker Sandboxes, Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, or Cloudflare."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"connection-adapters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Connection Adapters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI ships six built-in connection adapters plus a custom adapter interface:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  fetchServerSentEvents,\n  fetchHttpStream,\n  xhrServerSentEvents,\n  xhrHttpStream,\n  stream,\n  rpcStream,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { chatOnServer } from './server'\nimport { api } from './api'\n\n// Server-Sent Events (standard)\nfetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n// Raw HTTP streaming (newline-delimited JSON)\nfetchHttpStream('/api/chat')\n\n// XHR-based SSE / HTTP streaming (React Native / Expo, where fetch streaming is unavailable)\nxhrServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\nxhrHttpStream('/api/chat')\n\n// Direct async iterables (TanStack Start server functions)\nstream((messages) => chatOnServer({ messages }))\n\n// RPC-based transport\nrpcStream((messages, data) => api.streamResponse(messages, data))\n\n// Or implement your own ConnectionAdapter","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each adapter accepts static or dynamic (function-based) URLs and options. There's also a lighter-weight "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetcher"},{"type":"text","value":" option on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" for wiring a server function directly without a full adapter. Swap transport without changing application code. Vercel AI SDK centers on its SSE-based data stream protocol and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatTransport"},{"type":"text","value":" interface for extensibility, but doesn't ship the same breadth of built-in adapters - notably the XHR variants for React Native."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"extend-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extend Adapter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you use fine-tuned models, OpenAI-compatible proxies, or custom model endpoints, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extendAdapter()"},{"type":"text","value":" lets you add them to any provider adapter with full type safety:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { extendAdapter, createModel } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst customModels = [\n  createModel('my-fine-tuned-gpt4', ['text', 'image']),\n  createModel('company-internal-llm', ['text']),\n] as const\n\nconst myOpenai = extendAdapter(openaiText, customModels)\n\n// Full autocomplete - original models + custom models\nconst adapter = myOpenai('my-fine-tuned-gpt4')","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your custom models appear in autocomplete alongside official ones. Vercel AI SDK covers the registration half of this with the now-stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"customProvider()"},{"type":"text","value":" (custom and aliased model ids, settings overrides) and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createProviderRegistry()"},{"type":"text","value":"; the difference is type-safety depth - registry model ids are plain strings, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extendAdapter()"},{"type":"text","value":" gives custom models the same literal-type narrowing and per-model option gating as official ones."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI's middleware system hooks into every stage of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" lifecycle: configuration, streaming, tool execution, usage tracking, and completion. Each middleware is a plain object with named hooks that fire at specific phases."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, EventType, type ChatMiddleware, type StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst logger: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: 'logger',\n  onStart: (ctx) => {\n    console.log(`[${ctx.requestId}] Chat started`)\n  },\n  onChunk: (ctx, chunk) => {\n    // Transform, expand, or drop chunks. `type` is the discriminant, so it\n    // narrows `chunk` to the matching event and types `delta` as `string`.\n    if (chunk.type === EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT) {\n      return { ...chunk, delta: chunk.delta.replace(/\\b\\d{3}-\\d{2}-\\d{4}\\b/g, '[REDACTED]') }\n    }\n  },\n  onBeforeToolCall: (ctx, hookCtx) => {\n    // Intercept tool calls: transform args, skip, or abort\n    if (hookCtx.toolName === 'deleteDatabase') {\n      return { type: 'abort', reason: 'Dangerous operation blocked' }\n    }\n  },\n  onAfterToolCall: (ctx, info) => {\n    console.log(`${info.toolName}: ${info.ok ? 'success' : 'failed'} in ${info.duration}ms`)\n  },\n  onFinish: (ctx, info) => {\n    console.log(`Done in ${info.duration}ms, ${info.usage?.totalTokens} tokens`)\n  },\n}\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  middleware: [logger],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The available hooks cover the full lifecycle:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hook"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Purpose"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onConfig"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transform messages, tools, temperature, system prompts per iteration"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStructuredOutputConfig"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transform the structured-output schema/config before the call"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStart"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setup tasks (timers, logging)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onIteration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observe each agent-loop iteration"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onChunk"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transform, expand, or drop individual stream chunks"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onBeforeToolCall"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Intercept tool calls: transform args, skip execution, or abort the run"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onAfterToolCall"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observe tool results, timing, and errors"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onToolPhaseComplete"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observe the full batch of tool results for an iteration (e.g. aggregate approval state)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUsage"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Track token usage per iteration"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onAbort"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Terminal hooks (exactly one fires per run)"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware compose naturally. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" pipes through each middleware in order. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onChunk"},{"type":"text","value":" pipes chunks through each middleware (if one drops a chunk, later middleware never see it). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onBeforeToolCall"},{"type":"text","value":" uses first-win semantics: the first middleware that returns a decision short-circuits the rest."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI ships several built-in middleware. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolCacheMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"contentGuardMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" come from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai/middlewares"},{"type":"text","value":" subpath, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"otelMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai/middlewares/otel"},{"type":"text","value":" (kept on its own subpath so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@opentelemetry/api"},{"type":"text","value":" stays an optional peer). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolCacheMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":" caches tool results by name and arguments with configurable TTL, LRU eviction, and pluggable storage backends (Redis, localStorage, etc.)."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolCacheMiddleware, contentGuardMiddleware } from '@tanstack/ai/middlewares'\nimport { otelMiddleware } from '@tanstack/ai/middlewares/otel'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK takes a different approach: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wrapLanguageModel()"},{"type":"text","value":" wraps a model instance with middleware that can intercept and transform calls (and v6 adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wrapEmbeddingModel()"},{"type":"text","value":"). It ships several built-in middleware ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extractReasoningMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"simulateStreamingMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultSettingsMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":", and the new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"devToolsMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":"), but these all operate at the model level rather than the application level. v6 also exposes per-call options that cover slices of this surface: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_transform"},{"type":"text","value":" for stream transforms, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_onToolCallStart"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_onToolCallFinish"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prepareStep"},{"type":"text","value":" for per-step config changes, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_repairToolCall"},{"type":"text","value":". What it doesn't have is a unified middleware system at the application level - named, reusable middleware objects whose hooks span the whole lifecycle, compose in order, and can short-circuit tool calls with first-win semantics."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"no-platform-association","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No Platform Association"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI is a pure library. There's no optional platform layer, no gateway abstraction, no hosting-specific features, and no deployment-specific optimizations. Your AI code carries no implicit association with any deployment platform."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This isn't just philosophical - it means no accidental dependencies on platform-specific features, no gateway abstractions that subtly encourage vendor adoption, and no marketing surface embedded in your technical stack."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"code-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode lets the model write TypeScript that calls your tools inside a sandbox. One "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"execute_typescript"},{"type":"text","value":" call can loop, branch, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Promise.all"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of one tool per turn."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI ships six isolate drivers behind one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"IsolateDriver"},{"type":"text","value":" interface:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-isolate-node"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Node.js sandbox via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isolated-vm"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-isolate-quickjs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - QuickJS WASM (browsers and edge)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-isolate-quickjs-bun"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Native QuickJS on Bun via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bun:ffi"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-isolate-cloudflare"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Cloudflare Workers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-isolate-daytona"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Remote Daytona sandbox"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-isolate-e2b"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Remote E2B sandbox"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Swap the driver without changing application code. A companion "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-code-mode-snippets"},{"type":"text","value":" package gives the model a persistent snippet library. The model can save working TypeScript snippets, list them, and reuse them across sessions. Trust strategies control what gets promoted to a first-class tool."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK now ships experimental "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ai-sdk/code-mode"},{"type":"text","value":". It runs QuickJS only, needs Node 22 or newer, and does not run in the browser or on the edge. Nested tool approvals are rejected. Provider-hosted code execution (Anthropic, xAI, OpenAI) is still a separate path. None of those give the model a persistent, provider-agnostic skill library it builds itself. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../code-mode/code-mode","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"coding-agent-sandboxes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coding Agent Sandboxes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separately from the JS isolates above, TanStack AI can put a full coding-agent CLI inside an isolated sandbox with a real filesystem, a shell, and a cloned repo. The agent can be Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, or any ACP-compliant agent. Its work streams back through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" like any other run."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sandboxed run composes three swappable pieces:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"provider"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (where it runs)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (what the agent sees)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"harness adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (which agent runs)"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sandbox is a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" middleware, so the agent's edits and commands arrive as the same AG-UI stream every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" UI already renders."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo, withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'TanStack/ai' }),\n    packageManager: 'pnpm',\n  }),\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  middleware: [withSandbox(sandbox)],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two axes are open where the AI SDK's is narrower:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any agent, not a fixed list."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode ship as first-party harness packages, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acpCompatible"},{"type":"text","value":" (from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-acp"},{"type":"text","value":") turns "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"any"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"link","href":"https://agentclientprotocol.com","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Agent Client Protocol"}]},{"type":"text","value":" agent ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pi"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gemini --acp"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/agents","children":[{"type":"text","value":"dozens of others"}]},{"type":"text","value":") into a harness by describing how to launch it. Adding an agent does not require a dedicated adapter to exist."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any sandbox, not one cloud."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The same run executes on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localProcessSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" (host dev loop), "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dockerSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" (container), "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" (Docker Sandboxes microVM), Daytona, Vercel Sandbox, Sprites, or Cloudflare. Swap the provider without touching the harness or workspace. Providers declare their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"capabilities()"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fs"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"exec"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ports"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshots"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fork"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableFilesystem"},{"type":"text","value":", and more) so code degrades across them."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK 7 added an experimental "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HarnessAgent"},{"type":"text","value":" API for the same idea: run a coding-agent harness in a sandbox and return AI SDK "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generate()"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":" results. It ships adapters for Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, Deep Agents, and Pi. The documented path runs them in Vercel Sandbox, with long work on the Workflow SDK. There is no generic ACP escape hatch (each supported harness is its own package), and sandbox support centers on Vercel's own microVM rather than a provider-swappable contract. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sandbox providers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/harnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harnesses"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"media-generation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Media Generation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI provides stable, dedicated APIs for every media generation activity - image, video, speech, transcription, and summarization. Each is a separate, tree-shakeable function with its own adapter per provider."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK has added several of these capabilities. As of v6, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateImage()"},{"type":"text","value":" is stable; video generation is still experimental ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_generateVideo()"},{"type":"text","value":"); and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateSpeech()"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"transcribe()"},{"type":"text","value":" are exported without the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_"},{"type":"text","value":" prefix but are still documented as experimental features. TanStack AI's media APIs are stable across the board and go further in several areas:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Image generation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateImage()"},{"type":"text","value":" with per-model type safety. TypeScript knows that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gpt-image-2"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dall-e-3"},{"type":"text","value":" expose different size constraints. Five providers ship adapters: OpenAI (GPT Image, DALL-E), Gemini (Imagen), Grok, OpenRouter, and fal.ai (600+ community models including Flux, SDXL, and more)."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { generateImage } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst result = await generateImage({\n  adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n  prompt: 'A sunset over mountains',\n  size: '1536x1024',\n  numberOfImages: 1,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Video generation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateVideo()"},{"type":"text","value":" handles the full async job lifecycle automatically. Video generation APIs are inherently asynchronous - you submit a job, poll for status, and eventually get a result. TanStack AI manages this entire lifecycle with configurable polling intervals and timeouts, streaming status updates back to the client."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { generateVideo } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiVideo } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst stream = generateVideo({\n  adapter: openaiVideo('sora-2'),\n  prompt: 'A cat playing piano',\n  size: '1280x720',\n  duration: 8,\n  stream: true,          // Stream job lifecycle events\n  pollingInterval: 2000, // Poll every 2 seconds\n})\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  // Receive: job created → status updates → final video URL\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_generateVideo()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the video directly without exposing the job lifecycle or streaming status updates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text-to-speech"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateSpeech()"},{"type":"text","value":" supports 6 audio output formats (mp3, opus, aac, flac, wav, pcm), speed control (0.25x to 4x), and five providers: OpenAI (11 voices), Gemini (30+ voices with language hints), Grok, ElevenLabs, and fal.ai."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { generateSpeech } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiSpeech } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst result = await generateSpeech({\n  adapter: openaiSpeech('tts-1-hd'),\n  text: 'Hello, world!',\n  voice: 'nova',\n  format: 'opus',\n  speed: 1.2,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transcription"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateTranscription()"},{"type":"text","value":" supports common output formats (json, text, srt, verbose_json, vtt), word-level timestamps with confidence scores, and four providers (OpenAI, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai), with speaker diarization via OpenAI's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gpt-4o-transcribe-diarize"},{"type":"text","value":" model."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { generateTranscription } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiTranscription } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { audioFile } from './audio'\n\nconst result = await generateTranscription({\n  adapter: openaiTranscription('gpt-4o-transcribe'),\n  audio: audioFile,\n  responseFormat: 'verbose_json', // Includes word-level timestamps\n})\n\n// result.words → [{ word: 'Hello', start: 0.0, end: 0.42 }, ...]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Audio & music generation"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateAudio()"},{"type":"text","value":" generates music and sound effects across Gemini (Lyria), ElevenLabs (music + sound effects), and fal.ai. Vercel AI SDK has no equivalent."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Summarization"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"summarize()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a dedicated activity with style control ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bullet-points"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paragraph"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"concise"},{"type":"text","value":"), focus topics, and streaming support. Vercel AI SDK has no equivalent - summarization requires calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateText()"},{"type":"text","value":" with a prompt."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Realtime voice"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"realtimeToken()"},{"type":"text","value":" enables bidirectional audio streaming with Voice Activity Detection modes (server, semantic, manual), tool calling during voice sessions, and simultaneous audio + text output. Three providers ship realtime adapters: OpenAI (Realtime API), Grok, and ElevenLabs. Vercel AI SDK now ships experimental "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_useRealtime"},{"type":"text","value":" for OpenAI, Google, and xAI (plus AI Gateway). TanStack AI's realtime adapters are stable and include ElevenLabs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All media activities follow the same adapter pattern as chat - tree-shakeable imports, per-model type safety, and streaming support. If your app only uses chat, none of this media code enters your bundle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"native-ag-ui-protocol","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native AG-UI Protocol"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The events TanStack AI streams between server and client are "},{"type":"link","href":"https://docs.ag-ui.com/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AG-UI"}]},{"type":"text","value":" events ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_STARTED"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TEXT_MESSAGE_*"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TOOL_CALL_*"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":"), imported directly from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ag-ui/core"},{"type":"text","value":" - not a bespoke format with an AG-UI export bolted on. Anything that speaks AG-UI can sit on either side of the wire: AG-UI-compliant agent frameworks behind a TanStack AI frontend, or a TanStack AI client in front of an agent server written in another language entirely."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK streams its own proprietary UI Message Stream protocol. AG-UI interop requires an external translation layer ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ag-ui/vercel-ai-sdk"},{"type":"text","value":", built and maintained by the AG-UI project), and native support remains an open feature request on the AI SDK repo."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"hooks-for-every-activity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hooks for Every Activity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chat isn't the only activity with a hook. Every activity ships one - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useGeneration"},{"type":"text","value":" (streaming structured output), "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useGenerateImage"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useGenerateAudio"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useGenerateSpeech"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTranscription"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSummarize"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useGenerateVideo"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useRealtimeChat"},{"type":"text","value":" - with the same connection-adapter wiring and devtools integration as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":", across React, Solid, Vue, Svelte, Preact, and Angular."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK's UI layer has three hooks: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useCompletion"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useObject"},{"type":"text","value":". Its media functions ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateImage()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"experimental_generateVideo()"},{"type":"text","value":", speech, transcription) are server-side only - surfacing them in a UI means hand-rolling your own routes and client state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"multi-turn-structured-output","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-Turn Structured Output"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI preserves structured output in conversation history instead of leaving it only on a call result. Providers may produce it in the agent loop or through separate finalization; both paths create a typed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StructuredOutputPart"},{"type":"text","value":", streamed as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"partial"},{"type":"text","value":" and completed as a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"final"},{"type":"text","value":", with the schema generic threading all the way down to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages[i].parts[j].data"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK's structured output ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generateObject"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"streamObject"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Output"},{"type":"text","value":") is per-call: the typed object lives on the call result, the message-part union has no structured-output type, and combining "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" with typed structured output means manually parsing model text into custom data parts."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"debug-logging","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debug Logging"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"debug: true"},{"type":"text","value":" on any activity and the pipeline prints itself: raw provider chunks, post-middleware output, middleware hook inputs and outputs, tool execution, agent-loop iterations, config transforms, and request metadata - each category individually toggleable, with a pluggable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"logger"},{"type":"text","value":" for structured output. Vercel AI SDK's built-in logging covers provider warnings; richer observability goes through experimental telemetry hooks or the dev-only DevTools recorder rather than a debug log you can flip on anywhere."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"community-adapter-ecosystem","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Community Adapter Ecosystem"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI publishes an open adapter specification. Official LLM adapters:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Vertex, Grok, Groq, OpenRouter, Ollama"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bedrock, BytePlus, Mistral, Cohere, ElevenLabs, fal"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI Gateway, and any OpenAI-compatible endpoint via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"openaiCompatible"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness adapters: Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, and any ACP agent via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"acpCompatible"},{"type":"text","value":". The community has already built adapters for Decart, Cencori, Cloudflare, Soniox, and Mynth, with a "},{"type":"link","href":"../community-adapters/guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"guide for building your own"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-vercel-ai-sdk-excels","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where Vercel AI SDK Excels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provider breadth."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Vercel AI SDK ships ~38 first-party, individually typed provider packages, plus a large community list. If you want a specific provider as a dedicated, maintained package without writing an adapter, their coverage is still broader today. Raw model "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"count"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is not the differentiator: TanStack AI's OpenRouter adapter reaches OpenRouter's full catalog, the first-party Vercel Gateway adapter routes with one key, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"openaiCompatible"},{"type":"text","value":" connects to any OpenAI-compatible endpoint."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid vs Angular."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Both SDKs now ship official Angular. Solid still cuts the other way: AI SDK's Solid package is community-maintained and pinned to an older SDK major, while TanStack AI ships an official, current Solid integration."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Agent abstraction."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Vercel AI SDK ships a dedicated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Agent"},{"type":"text","value":" abstraction (the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolLoopAgent"},{"type":"text","value":" class) that packages a model, tools, instructions, and loop settings into a reusable object with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".generate()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".stream()"},{"type":"text","value":" methods, plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InferAgentUIMessage"},{"type":"text","value":" for end-to-end type safety. TanStack AI composes these pieces per call rather than offering a single agent class."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AI Gateway."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Vercel's optional AI Gateway adds centralized provider management: failover routing, caching, and a single key across providers, integrated with the Vercel platform (and used by default when no provider is configured). TanStack AI ships no gateway of its own. For the same centralized routing, use the first-class "},{"type":"link","href":"../adapters/openrouter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenRouter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" adapter or the "},{"type":"link","href":"../adapters/vercel-gateway","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI Gateway"}]},{"type":"text","value":" adapter. Neither attaches a platform layer to the rest of your stack."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Server Components."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Vercel AI SDK has an RSC integration via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ai-sdk/rsc"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AIState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamableValue"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"streamUI"},{"type":"text","value":"). Note that Vercel documents this as experimental and recommends AI SDK UI for production - so it's an option for Next.js RSC apps rather than the primary path."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"side-by-side-key-differences","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Side-by-Side: Key Differences"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tool-definition","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Definition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Isomorphic definitions with separate runtime implementations:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { weatherApi } from './weather'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Get current weather for a location',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temp: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n})\n\n// Server implementation\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeather.server(async ({ city }) => {\n  const data = await weatherApi.get(city)\n  return { temp: data.temperature, condition: data.condition }\n})\n\n// Client implementation\nconst getWeatherClient = getWeather.client(async ({ city }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/weather?city=${city}`)\n  return res.json()\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Tool objects via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tool()"},{"type":"text","value":" helper:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { generateText, tool } from 'ai'\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { weatherApi } from './weather'\n\nconst result = await generateText({\n  model: openai('gpt-5.5'),\n  tools: {\n    getWeather: tool({\n      description: 'Get current weather for a location',\n      inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n      execute: async ({ city }) => {\n        const data = await weatherApi.get(city)\n        return { temp: data.temperature, condition: data.condition }\n      },\n    }),\n  },\n  prompt: \"What's the weather in Tokyo?\",\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The TanStack approach separates the tool contract from its implementation, making tools reusable across server and client contexts."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"persistence-2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Middleware writes the transcript. The client reloads it."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  threadId: 'support-chat',\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  persistence: true,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - You own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveChat"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadChat"},{"type":"text","value":" and call them from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onEnd"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { convertToModelMessages, createUIMessageStreamResponse, streamText, toUIMessageStream } from 'ai'\nimport { loadChat, saveChat } from './chat-store'\n\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { message, id } = await req.json()\n  const messages = [...(await loadChat(id)), message]\n\n  const result = streamText({\n    model: 'openai/gpt-5.5',\n    messages: await convertToModelMessages(messages),\n  })\n\n  return createUIMessageStreamResponse({\n    stream: toUIMessageStream({\n      stream: result.stream,\n      originalMessages: messages,\n      onEnd: ({ messages: next }) => {\n        saveChat({ chatId: id, messages: next })\n      },\n    }),\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI also persists run status and pending approvals, and it can restore a long media generation into the same hook fields. Vercel AI SDK leaves that wiring to you. Resume of a live stream is a separate layer on both sides: TanStack AI uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":", Vercel AI SDK uses Redis plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumable-stream"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"agent-loop-control","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Agent Loop Control"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Composable strategies:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, combineStrategies, maxIterations, untilFinishReason } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\nimport { estimatedTokens } from './cost'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Help me plan a trip.' }]\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools,\n  agentLoopStrategy: combineStrategies([\n    maxIterations(10),\n    untilFinishReason(['stop']),\n    ({ iterationCount }) => estimatedTokens(iterationCount) < 50_000,\n  ]),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopWhen"},{"type":"text","value":" conditions (v5+):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { generateText, stepCountIs } from 'ai'\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\n\nconst result = await generateText({\n  model: openai('gpt-5.5'),\n  tools,\n  stopWhen: stepCountIs(10), // also: hasToolCall('name'), or a custom function\n  prompt: 'Help me plan a trip.',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both let you compose multiple stopping conditions - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stopWhen"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts an array of conditions including custom functions, and v6 adds a reusable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Agent"},{"type":"text","value":" class. The remaining nuance is ergonomic: TanStack AI's strategies are plain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(state) => boolean"},{"type":"text","value":" predicates combined with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"combineStrategies"},{"type":"text","value":", so token budgets and custom business logic are first-class without reaching for a built-in condition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tree-shaking","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tree-Shaking"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Separate adapters per activity:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Only bundles chat + OpenAI text adapter\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel AI SDK"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Single provider import:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Provider package includes all model types\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In TanStack AI, each activity (chat, image, speech, video, transcription, summarization) is a separate adapter function. You import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"openaiText"},{"type":"text","value":" for chat and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"openaiImage"},{"type":"text","value":" for image generation - they're independent modules. Vercel AI SDK's provider packages are more monolithic."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-choose-tanstack-ai","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Choose TanStack AI"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bundle size matters"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Tree-shakeable adapters per activity mean smaller bundles"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AG-UI native"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - The wire protocol is AG-UI end to end; interoperate with the agent-UI ecosystem and non-TypeScript agent servers without a translation layer"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Solid, Preact, Angular, or React Native"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - One headless core covers React, Solid, Vue, Svelte, Preact, Angular, and React Native (via XHR adapters), all officially maintained"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hooks beyond chat"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useGeneration"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useGenerateImage"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSummarize"},{"type":"text","value":", and the rest of the generation hook family across every supported framework"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isomorphic tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Define a tool once and derive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".server()"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".client()"},{"type":"text","value":" implementations from one contract"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"App-level middleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Lifecycle hooks for chunks, tool calls, usage, and errors - not just model wrapping"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chat and generation persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":", or a browser store; long media runs restore through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - A durability adapter on the response; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" reconnects without Redis"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable sandbox runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Detach, journal, takeover, and reaper so a coding agent survives a closed tab"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Realtime voice"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Stable bidirectional audio across OpenAI, Grok, and ElevenLabs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No vendor association"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Pure library with no platform layer"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-model type safety"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - TypeScript narrows options per model, not per provider"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code Mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Five isolate drivers (Node, QuickJS WASM, QuickJS Bun, Cloudflare, Daytona) plus an LLM-writable skill library"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coding agent sandboxes"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Run Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, or any ACP agent in a swappable sandbox (local, Docker, Docker Sandboxes, Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Cloudflare), streamed through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flexible transport"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - SSE, HTTP streams, XHR, RPC, direct iterables, or custom adapters"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MCP, two ways"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - A standalone host-side client ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-mcp"},{"type":"text","value":") with pools, codegen, and managed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" lifecycle, plus a provider-routed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mcpTool()"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-choose-vercel-ai-sdk","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Choose Vercel AI SDK"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Need a first-party package for a specific provider"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - ~38 dedicated, individually typed provider packages today (TanStack reaches comparable model breadth via OpenRouter, Vercel Gateway, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"openaiCompatible"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Agent abstraction"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - A reusable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Agent"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ToolLoopAgent"},{"type":"text","value":") class with end-to-end UI message types"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel platform"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - AI Gateway, observability, and deployment optimization"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Server Components"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - RSC primitives via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@ai-sdk/rsc"},{"type":"text","value":" (experimental; AI SDK UI is the recommended production path)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workflow-backed long runs on Vercel"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - experimental "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HarnessAgent"},{"type":"text","value":" plus the Workflow SDK, if your stack is already that platform"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"getting-started","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Getting Started"}]},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"package-manager","mode":"install"},"children":[],"properties":{"data-package-manager-meta":"{\"packagesByFramework\":{\"react\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-react\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"vue\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-vue\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"solid\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-solid\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"svelte\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-svelte\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"preact\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-preact\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"angular\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-angular\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"vanilla\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-client\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"octane\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-octane\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\",\"octane\"]]},\"mode\":\"install\"}"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../getting-started/quick-start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Start Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to build your first chat application, or explore the "},{"type":"link","href":"../getting-started/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"full documentation"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"feature-comparison","text":"Feature Comparison","level":2},{"id":"where-tanstack-ai-excels","text":"Where TanStack AI Excels","level":2},{"id":"per-model-type-safety","text":"Per-Model Type Safety","level":3},{"id":"tree-shakeable-adapters","text":"Tree-Shakeable Adapters","level":3},{"id":"isomorphic-tools","text":"Isomorphic Tools","level":3},{"id":"composable-agent-loop-strategies","text":"Composable Agent Loop Strategies","level":3},{"id":"lazy-tool-discovery","text":"Lazy Tool Discovery","level":3},{"id":"model-context-protocol-mcp","text":"Model Context Protocol (MCP)","level":3},{"id":"mcp-apps-interactive-widgets","text":"MCP Apps (Interactive Widgets)","level":3},{"id":"headless-client-architecture","text":"Headless Client Architecture","level":3},{"id":"persistence","text":"Persistence","level":3},{"id":"resumable-streams","text":"Resumable Streams","level":3},{"id":"durable-sandbox-runs","text":"Durable Sandbox Runs","level":3},{"id":"connection-adapters","text":"Connection Adapters","level":3},{"id":"extend-adapter","text":"Extend Adapter","level":3},{"id":"middleware","text":"Middleware","level":3},{"id":"no-platform-association","text":"No Platform Association","level":3},{"id":"code-mode","text":"Code Mode","level":3},{"id":"coding-agent-sandboxes","text":"Coding Agent Sandboxes","level":3},{"id":"media-generation","text":"Media Generation","level":3},{"id":"native-ag-ui-protocol","text":"Native AG-UI Protocol","level":3},{"id":"hooks-for-every-activity","text":"Hooks for Every Activity","level":3},{"id":"multi-turn-structured-output","text":"Multi-Turn Structured Output","level":3},{"id":"debug-logging","text":"Debug Logging","level":3},{"id":"community-adapter-ecosystem","text":"Community Adapter Ecosystem","level":3},{"id":"where-vercel-ai-sdk-excels","text":"Where Vercel AI SDK Excels","level":2},{"id":"side-by-side-key-differences","text":"Side-by-Side: Key Differences","level":2},{"id":"tool-definition","text":"Tool Definition","level":3},{"id":"persistence-2","text":"Persistence","level":3},{"id":"agent-loop-control","text":"Agent Loop Control","level":3},{"id":"tree-shaking","text":"Tree-Shaking","level":3},{"id":"when-to-choose-tanstack-ai","text":"When to Choose TanStack AI","level":2},{"id":"when-to-choose-vercel-ai-sdk","text":"When to Choose Vercel AI SDK","level":2},{"id":"getting-started","text":"Getting Started","level":2}]},"frameworks":["react","vue","solid","svelte","preact","angular","vanilla","octane"],"downloadMarkdown":"\nBoth TanStack AI and Vercel AI SDK are open-source TypeScript toolkits for building AI-powered applications. They share common ground - streaming chat, tool calling, multi-provider support, and deploy-anywhere flexibility - but they approach the problem from fundamentally different directions.\n\nTanStack AI treats AI as a **library composition problem**. Every piece - adapters, tools, agent loops, transport, UI - is a composable building block. You import what you need, compose it how you want, and ship it wherever you want. No platform layer, no gateway abstraction, no implicit associations.\n\nVercel AI SDK treats AI as a **full-stack platform problem**. It provides a broad surface area of primitives with optional platform integration for gateway routing, observability, and deployment optimization.\n\nThis article compares the two SDKs from TanStack AI's perspective, with honest acknowledgment of where each excels.\n\n## Feature Comparison\n\nVersions referenced below: TanStack AI as of this writing; Vercel AI SDK `ai@7.x` (v7 is current).\n\n| Feature | TanStack AI | Vercel AI SDK |\n|---------|------------|---------------|\n| License | MIT | Apache 2.0 |\n| Hosting | Works anywhere | Works anywhere |\n| Providers | 16 official LLM adapters (OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Vertex, Grok, Groq, OpenRouter, Ollama, Bedrock, BytePlus, Mistral, Cohere, ElevenLabs, fal, Vercel Gateway, `openaiCompatible`) plus 5 harness adapters; community adapters for more | ~38 first-party provider packages (plus community); 100+ models via AI Gateway |\n| Framework Hooks | React, Solid, Svelte, Vue, Preact, Angular (+ React Native) | React, Vue, Svelte, Angular (Solid is community-maintained) |\n| Generation UI Hooks | One hook per activity: chat, structured output, image, audio, speech, transcription, summarize, video, realtime | `useChat`, `useCompletion`, `useObject` |\n| Wire Protocol | Native AG-UI events end to end | Proprietary UI Message Stream; AG-UI via external translation layer |\n| Streaming | Built-in with configurable chunk strategies | Built-in with progressive delivery |\n| Chat Persistence | `withPersistence` middleware + `reconstructChat`; server store or browser stores (`localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, IndexedDB) | Guide: you own `saveChat` / `loadChat` / `validateUIMessages` |\n| Generation Persistence | `withGenerationPersistence` restores long media runs into the same hook fields | - |\n| Resumable Streams | `StreamDurability` in core; `memoryStream` or `durableStream`; `useChat` reconnects on its own | Redis + `resumable-stream` + `activeStreamId`; you wire POST and GET |\n| Durable Sandbox Runs | Detach, journal, takeover, reaper; the agent keeps working after the tab closes | `HarnessAgent` + Workflow SDK (experimental); centered on Vercel Sandbox |\n| Tool Calling | Isomorphic `.server()` / `.client()` system | `tool()` objects; client execution via `onToolCall` |\n| Agent Loop Control | Composable strategy functions `(state) => boolean` | `stopWhen` conditions + `Agent` (`ToolLoopAgent`) class |\n| Tool Approval | Per-tool `needsApproval` with batched approval flow | Per-tool `needsApproval` (human-in-the-loop) |\n| Type Safety | Per-model type narrowing | Per-provider types |\n| Tree-Shaking | Separate adapter per activity (text, image, speech, etc.) | Monolithic provider packages |\n| Lazy Tool Discovery | Built-in - works across every provider | Anthropic-only tool search with `deferLoading` (provider-hosted) |\n| Connection Adapters | SSE, HTTP stream, XHR (SSE/stream), RPC, direct async iterables, `fetcher`, custom | SSE-based data stream protocol (`ChatTransport`) |\n| Middleware | App-level lifecycle hooks (config, iterations, chunks, tool calls, usage, errors) | Model-level wrapping via `wrapLanguageModel()` |\n| Extend Adapter | Add custom/fine-tuned models with per-model type narrowing | `customProvider()` / `createProviderRegistry()` (string model ids) |\n| Structured Outputs | Typed `StructuredOutputPart`, streamed alongside tools and preserved per turn in message history | `generateObject()` / `streamObject()` / `Output` API (per-call; no structured-output message part) |\n| Image Generation | Stable API with per-model type safety (OpenAI, Gemini, Grok, OpenRouter, fal.ai) | `generateImage()` (stable) |\n| Video Generation | Stable API with async job lifecycle (OpenAI, fal.ai) | `experimental_generateVideo()` |\n| Text-to-Speech | Stable API, 6 output formats, speed control (OpenAI, Gemini, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai) | `generateSpeech()` (experimental) |\n| Transcription | Stable API with word timestamps and diarization (OpenAI, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai) | `transcribe()` (experimental) |\n| Audio / Music Generation | `generateAudio()` for music & sound effects (Gemini, ElevenLabs, fal.ai) | - |\n| Summarization | Dedicated `summarize()` with streaming and style options | - |\n| Code Mode | 5 isolate drivers you run yourself: Node `isolated-vm`, QuickJS WASM, QuickJS Bun, Cloudflare, Daytona | Experimental `@ai-sdk/code-mode` (QuickJS only, Node 22+, not browser or edge); also provider-hosted code-execution tools |\n| Code Mode Skills | LLM-writable persistent skill library | Static skill uploads; no model-built library |\n| Coding Agent Sandboxes | First-party Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode + any ACP agent via `acpCompatible`; local-process, Docker, Docker Sandboxes (`sbx`), Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Cloudflare | `HarnessAgent` (experimental): Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, Deep Agents, Pi; centered on Vercel Sandbox |\n| Realtime Voice | Stable: OpenAI, Grok, and ElevenLabs with VAD modes and tool support | Experimental `experimental_useRealtime` (OpenAI, Google, xAI + Gateway) |\n| DevTools | Isomorphic in-app panel via TanStack DevTools (all frameworks, media previews) | `devToolsMiddleware` + local inspector (server-side, dev-only) |\n| Debug Logging | One flag, per-category toggles, pluggable logger | Warning logs + experimental telemetry hooks |\n| MCP Client | Standalone host-side client (`@tanstack/ai-mcp`) + provider-routed `mcpTool()` | Built-in (`@ai-sdk/mcp`, stable) |\n| MCP Apps (Interactive Widgets) | `ui://` widgets via `@mcp-ui/client`; React + Preact + framework-agnostic bridge; multi-server routing, pluggable session store, link-scheme hardening | `experimental_MCPAppRenderer` (React only); model-vs-app tool split; iframe sandbox + allowlist |\n| Platform Association | None - pure library | Optional Vercel integration |\n\n## Where TanStack AI Excels\n\n### Per-Model Type Safety\n\nWhen you select a provider and model, TypeScript narrows the exact options, capabilities, and input modalities available for that specific model - not a union of everything the provider supports.\n\nEach provider adapter contains a comprehensive `model-meta.ts` that maps every model to its capabilities: supported input modalities, context windows, and provider-specific options. When you write `openaiText('gpt-5.5')`, the type system knows exactly what that model can do.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\n// TypeScript knows gpt-5.5 supports text + image input\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{\n    role: 'user',\n    content: [\n      { type: 'text', content: 'What is in this image?' },\n      { type: 'image', source: { type: 'url', value: 'https://example.com/photo.jpg' } },\n    ],\n  }],\n})\n```\n\nIf you pass an image content part to a text-only model, TypeScript catches it at compile time.\n\n### Tree-Shakeable Adapters\n\nEvery AI activity - chat, summarization, image generation, speech, transcription, video - is a separate import. Every provider exposes separate adapter functions per activity. If your app only uses chat, image generation code never enters your bundle.\n\n```ts ignore\n// Only chat code is bundled - nothing else\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\n// vs. importing activities you actually need\nimport { chat, generateImage } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText, openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n```\n\nThis is architectural, not incidental. Each adapter implements a specific interface (`TextAdapter`, `ImageAdapter`, `TTSAdapter`, etc.) and lives in its own module. Modern bundlers eliminate everything you don't import.\n\n### Isomorphic Tools\n\n`toolDefinition()` creates a shared contract - name, description, input schema, output schema - that can be implemented for different runtimes. `.server()` adds a server-side implementation with access to databases and APIs. `.client()` adds a client-side implementation that runs in the browser.\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\n// Define once - shared validation contract\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'addToCart',\n  description: 'Add an item to the shopping cart',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n})\n\n// Server implementation - database access\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async ({ itemId, quantity }) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.addItem(itemId, quantity)\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id }\n})\n\n// Client implementation - runs in the browser\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client(async ({ itemId, quantity }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/cart`, {\n    method: 'POST',\n    body: JSON.stringify({ itemId, quantity }),\n  })\n  return res.json()\n})\n```\n\nThe same schema validates inputs and outputs on both sides. The type system tracks whether a tool is a `ServerTool` or `ClientTool` at compile time.\n\nVercel AI SDK defines tools with a `tool()` helper and does support client-side execution - a tool with no `execute` function is handled in the browser via the UI hook's `onToolCall` callback, with the result returned through `addToolOutput` (renamed from `addToolResult` in v6). What it doesn't have is a single shared contract that produces separate `.server()` and `.client()` implementations: server and client tool code are declared independently rather than derived from one definition.\n\n### Composable Agent Loop Strategies\n\nTanStack AI provides agent loop control as composable pure functions. Each strategy is `(state) => boolean` - return `true` to continue, `false` to stop.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, maxIterations, untilFinishReason, combineStrategies } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Help me plan a trip.' }]\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools,\n  agentLoopStrategy: combineStrategies([\n    maxIterations(10),\n    untilFinishReason(['stop', 'length']),\n  ]),\n})\n```\n\n`combineStrategies` composes them with AND logic - all strategies must agree to continue. You can add custom strategies alongside built-in ones:\n\n```ts\nimport { maxIterations, untilFinishReason, combineStrategies } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { estimatedCost, budget } from './cost'\n\ncombineStrategies([\n  maxIterations(10),\n  untilFinishReason(['stop']),\n  // Custom: stop if budget exceeded\n  ({ iterationCount }) => estimatedCost(iterationCount) < budget,\n])\n```\n\nVercel AI SDK (v5+) controls agent loops via `stopWhen`, which accepts composable stopping conditions like `stepCountIs(n)` and `hasToolCall(name)` (the default is `stepCountIs(20)`), and v6 adds a dedicated `Agent` abstraction (the `ToolLoopAgent` class) that bundles model, tools, instructions, and loop settings into a reusable object. The remaining difference is in the composition model: TanStack AI's strategies are arbitrary `(state) => boolean` predicates you write inline and combine with `combineStrategies`, so a stopping condition can encode any business logic (token budgets, cost ceilings, custom state checks) without waiting for a built-in condition to exist. Vercel's `stopWhen` also accepts custom functions, so the gap here is smaller than it once was.\n\n### Lazy Tool Discovery\n\nWhen your application has dozens of tools, sending all their schemas to the LLM on every request wastes tokens. TanStack AI solves this with lazy tool discovery.\n\nMark tools as `lazy: true` and they won't be sent to the LLM initially. Instead, a synthetic discovery tool is injected that lets the LLM request tool schemas on demand:\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst searchProducts = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'searchProducts',\n  description: 'Search the product catalog',\n  lazy: true, // Not sent to LLM initially\n  inputSchema: z.object({ query: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ id: z.string(), name: z.string() })),\n})\n```\n\nThe LLM sees a lightweight `__lazy__tool__discovery__` tool listing available tool names. When it needs one, it calls the discovery tool to get the full schema, then uses the real tool. For applications with large tool inventories, this significantly reduces per-request token costs.\n\nVercel AI SDK 6 added a provider-specific analogue for Anthropic: the tool search provider tool (`toolSearchBm25` / `toolSearchRegex`) with per-tool `deferLoading`, where deferred tools are excluded from the initial prompt and discovered on demand. It's Anthropic-only and runs provider-side; TanStack AI's lazy discovery works across every provider and runs in your own agent loop.\n\n### Model Context Protocol (MCP)\n\nTanStack AI connects to MCP servers two ways, and you can mix them in a single `chat()` run:\n\n- **Host-side client** (`@tanstack/ai-mcp`) - your server connects directly to any MCP server. `createMCPClient` (single server) and `createMCPClients` (multi-server pool) discover and execute tools, read resources, and fetch prompts over Streamable HTTP, SSE, or stdio transports, with OAuth 2.1 (`authProvider`) and static-token auth.\n- **Provider-routed** (`mcpTool()`) - the *provider* connects to the MCP server on your behalf (OpenAI Responses API, Anthropic), so no MCP traffic flows through your server at all.\n\nThe host-side client goes beyond basic discovery:\n\n- **Managed lifecycle** - hand clients to `chat()` via the `mcp` option and it discovers tools and closes connections when the run ends - no `try/finally` per route.\n- **Multi-server pools** - `createMCPClients` connects to many servers in parallel, auto-prefixing each server's tools to prevent name collisions.\n- **Three modes of type safety** - untyped auto-discovery, `toolDefinition()`-typed allowlists with Zod validation, or fully generated per-server types via the `tanstack-ai-mcp` CLI.\n- **Lazy discovery** - `tools({ lazy: true })` defers sending tool schemas to the LLM, plugging into TanStack AI's lazy tool discovery to cut token usage on tool-heavy servers.\n- **Resources & prompts** - inject MCP resources and prompts into a run with `mcpResourceToContentPart` and `mcpPromptToMessages`.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'What tools are available?' }]\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\n\n// chat() discovers the tools and closes the client when the run ends\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: { clients: [mcp] },\n})\n```\n\n`createMCPClient` tries protocol `2026-07-28` first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake.\n\n- A client app imports `@modelcontextprotocol/client`.\n- A server app imports `@modelcontextprotocol/server`.\n\nPackage names for an `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` import are in [MCP SDK packages](../migration/mcp-sdk).\n\nVercel AI SDK's `@ai-sdk/mcp` (`createMCPClient`) is a stable host-side client with HTTP/SSE transports, OAuth, resource reading, and prompt templates. TanStack AI's `@tanstack/ai-mcp` matches that surface and adds generated end-to-end types, multi-server pools, lazy discovery, a managed `chat()` lifecycle, and the provider-routed `mcpTool()` alternative.\n\n### MCP Apps (Interactive Widgets)\n\nBoth SDKs implement [MCP Apps](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) — the ratified MCP extension (standardized 2026-01-26) where a server returns a `ui://` resource so a tool result renders as an interactive widget in a sandboxed iframe instead of raw JSON. Both keep the widget HTML out of model input and render it in a sandboxed iframe with a tool allowlist and safe link handling. TanStack AI's implementation is more built out along three axes:\n\n- **More than one framework.** Widgets render via `@tanstack/ai-react/mcp-apps` and `@tanstack/ai-preact/mcp-apps`, and the bridge that routes widget actions (`createMcpAppBridge`) lives in the framework-agnostic `@tanstack/ai-client`, so a new framework only needs a thin renderer. Vercel's `experimental_MCPAppRenderer` and its bridge live in `@ai-sdk/react` — React only.\n- **Multi-server routing.** Each `UIResourcePart` carries a `serverId` (the pool prefix from `createMCPClients`), and interactive calls route back to the exact server that produced the widget — automatically when you run a multi-server pool. The call handler also enforces an unconditional same-server exposure check (`toolName` must be a tool that server actually exposes) with an optional `allowTool` restriction AND-ed on top.\n- **Session persistence & serverless-safety.** The call handler reconnects per call from a transport descriptor (stateless, serverless-safe by default), and stateful transports opt into a pluggable `McpSessionStore` — an `inMemoryMcpSessionStore` ships, and SQL/KV backends drop in behind the same interface.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, useMcpAppBridge } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { MCPAppResource } from '@tanstack/ai-react/mcp-apps'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  // Routes widget tool-calls to /api/mcp-apps/call by serverId; only http/https/mailto links pass through.\n  const bridge = useMcpAppBridge({\n    threadId: 'weather-chat',\n    callEndpoint: '/api/mcp-apps/call',\n    chat: { sendMessage: async (content) => void sendMessage({ content }) },\n    onLink: (url) => window.open(url, '_blank', 'noopener'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {messages.map((m) =>\n        m.parts.map((part, i) =>\n          part.type === 'ui-resource' ? (\n            <MCPAppResource\n              key={i}\n              part={part}\n              bridge={bridge}\n              sandbox={{ url: new URL('https://your-app.example.com/mcp-sandbox.html') }}\n            />\n          ) : null,\n        ),\n      )}\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nVercel AI SDK 7 covers the core flow and adds one thing TanStack AI doesn't: `splitMCPAppTools`, which separates *model-visible* tools from *app-only* tools the widget can call but the model never sees. Both implementations are new — Vercel marks its renderer `experimental_`, and TanStack AI's writeback of widget tool-calls into chat history is still out of scope. See the [MCP Apps guide](../mcp/apps) for the full API.\n\n### Headless Client Architecture\n\n`ChatClient` is a framework-agnostic class that manages the entire chat lifecycle - streaming, message state, tool execution, approval flows, and connection management. Every framework integration wraps this single client:\n\n- `@tanstack/ai-react` - `useChat` hook wraps `ChatClient`\n- `@tanstack/ai-solid` - `useChat` hook wraps `ChatClient`\n- `@tanstack/ai-vue` - `useChat` composable wraps `ChatClient`\n- `@tanstack/ai-svelte` - `createChat` wraps `ChatClient` (Svelte 5 runes)\n- `@tanstack/ai-preact` - `useChat` hook wraps `ChatClient`\n- `@tanstack/ai-angular` - `injectChat` wraps `ChatClient`\n\nNo framework-specific logic in the core. If a new framework emerges, it only needs a thin reactive wrapper.\n\n`ChatClient` also accepts a persistence adapter (`ChatClientPersistence`) for saving and restoring conversations client-side, and a typed runtime `context` that flows through to tools and middleware.\n\n### Persistence\n\nA reload or a second device must not wipe the conversation. TanStack AI treats that as a first-class layer, not a guide you implement by hand.\n\n`withPersistence` writes the transcript, run status, and pending approvals into your store. The client has two modes:\n\n- **`persistence: true`** puts the server in charge. The browser caches nothing and asks the server for the thread on mount.\n- **`persistence: <adapter>`** puts the browser in charge, with `localStoragePersistence()`, `sessionStoragePersistence()`, or `indexedDBPersistence()`.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nfunction Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'support-chat',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n  return <button onClick={() => sendMessage('hi')}>{messages.length}</button>\n}\n```\n\nWith `persistence: true`, add a `GET` that calls `reconstructChat`. That handler returns the stored transcript. If you also pass a durability adapter, the same `GET` can resume a run that is still streaming. See [Persistence](../persistence/overview).\n\nVercel AI SDK documents message persistence as a pattern. You write `saveChat`, `loadChat`, and `validateUIMessages`. There is no middleware that owns the transcript for you.\n\nLong media runs (video, batch images) use a second middleware, `withGenerationPersistence`. A reload restores `status`, `result`, and `error` into the same generation hook. See [Generation Persistence](../persistence/generation-persistence).\n\n### Resumable Streams\n\nA dropped socket is a different problem from a missing transcript. The model already ran. You want the rest of the tokens, not a second bill.\n\nPass a durability adapter to `toServerSentEventsResponse`. The adapter records every chunk before delivery. On reconnect the client sends the last offset and the server replays the log.\n\n- **`memoryStream`** from `@tanstack/ai` keeps the log in process memory. Use it in development.\n- **`durableStream`** from `@tanstack/ai-durable-stream` writes to an external [Durable Streams](https://durablestreams.com) backend. Use it in production.\n\n`useChat` reconnects on its own. You add a `GET` that calls `resumeServerSentEventsResponse`.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n  })\n}\n\nexport function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n}\n```\n\nIn production, swap `memoryStream(request)` for `durableStream(request, options)`. Everything else stays the same. See [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview).\n\nVercel AI SDK resume needs Redis, the `resumable-stream` package, an `activeStreamId` you store, and two routes you write. The SDK gives `resume` on `useChat` and a `consumeSseStream` callback. The store and the wiring stay yours.\n\n### Durable Sandbox Runs\n\nA coding agent can run for ten minutes. The tab will close. The wifi will drop. The next request can land on a different host.\n\nWithout durability, TanStack AI destroys the sandbox on disconnect. That stops a runaway bill. With durability, the agent detaches, writes a journal at `/tmp/tanstack-runs/<runId>.ndjson`, and a later `GET` can take the run over.\n\nTurn it on with both `runs` and `durability` on `withSandbox`. Passing only one leaves the default destroy-on-disconnect behavior.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const adapter = memoryStream(request)\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        runs: persistence.stores.runs,\n        durability: { adapter },\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, { durability: { adapter } })\n}\n```\n\nSchedule the reaper, or sandboxes keep billing after no reader is left. Use a distributed lock across hosts. See [Durable Runs](../sandbox/durable-runs).\n\nVercel AI SDK runs long harness work through experimental `HarnessAgent` and the Workflow SDK, centered on Vercel Sandbox. TanStack AI uses the same `chat()` + `withSandbox` path on every provider: local process, Docker, Docker Sandboxes, Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, or Cloudflare.\n\n### Connection Adapters\n\nTanStack AI ships six built-in connection adapters plus a custom adapter interface:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  fetchServerSentEvents,\n  fetchHttpStream,\n  xhrServerSentEvents,\n  xhrHttpStream,\n  stream,\n  rpcStream,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { chatOnServer } from './server'\nimport { api } from './api'\n\n// Server-Sent Events (standard)\nfetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n// Raw HTTP streaming (newline-delimited JSON)\nfetchHttpStream('/api/chat')\n\n// XHR-based SSE / HTTP streaming (React Native / Expo, where fetch streaming is unavailable)\nxhrServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\nxhrHttpStream('/api/chat')\n\n// Direct async iterables (TanStack Start server functions)\nstream((messages) => chatOnServer({ messages }))\n\n// RPC-based transport\nrpcStream((messages, data) => api.streamResponse(messages, data))\n\n// Or implement your own ConnectionAdapter\n```\n\nEach adapter accepts static or dynamic (function-based) URLs and options. There's also a lighter-weight `fetcher` option on `ChatClient` / `useChat` for wiring a server function directly without a full adapter. Swap transport without changing application code. Vercel AI SDK centers on its SSE-based data stream protocol and a `ChatTransport` interface for extensibility, but doesn't ship the same breadth of built-in adapters - notably the XHR variants for React Native.\n\n### Extend Adapter\n\nWhen you use fine-tuned models, OpenAI-compatible proxies, or custom model endpoints, `extendAdapter()` lets you add them to any provider adapter with full type safety:\n\n```ts\nimport { extendAdapter, createModel } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst customModels = [\n  createModel('my-fine-tuned-gpt4', ['text', 'image']),\n  createModel('company-internal-llm', ['text']),\n] as const\n\nconst myOpenai = extendAdapter(openaiText, customModels)\n\n// Full autocomplete - original models + custom models\nconst adapter = myOpenai('my-fine-tuned-gpt4')\n```\n\nYour custom models appear in autocomplete alongside official ones. Vercel AI SDK covers the registration half of this with the now-stable `customProvider()` (custom and aliased model ids, settings overrides) and `createProviderRegistry()`; the difference is type-safety depth - registry model ids are plain strings, while `extendAdapter()` gives custom models the same literal-type narrowing and per-model option gating as official ones.\n\n### Middleware\n\nTanStack AI's middleware system hooks into every stage of the `chat()` lifecycle: configuration, streaming, tool execution, usage tracking, and completion. Each middleware is a plain object with named hooks that fire at specific phases.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, EventType, type ChatMiddleware, type StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst logger: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: 'logger',\n  onStart: (ctx) => {\n    console.log(`[${ctx.requestId}] Chat started`)\n  },\n  onChunk: (ctx, chunk) => {\n    // Transform, expand, or drop chunks. `type` is the discriminant, so it\n    // narrows `chunk` to the matching event and types `delta` as `string`.\n    if (chunk.type === EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT) {\n      return { ...chunk, delta: chunk.delta.replace(/\\b\\d{3}-\\d{2}-\\d{4}\\b/g, '[REDACTED]') }\n    }\n  },\n  onBeforeToolCall: (ctx, hookCtx) => {\n    // Intercept tool calls: transform args, skip, or abort\n    if (hookCtx.toolName === 'deleteDatabase') {\n      return { type: 'abort', reason: 'Dangerous operation blocked' }\n    }\n  },\n  onAfterToolCall: (ctx, info) => {\n    console.log(`${info.toolName}: ${info.ok ? 'success' : 'failed'} in ${info.duration}ms`)\n  },\n  onFinish: (ctx, info) => {\n    console.log(`Done in ${info.duration}ms, ${info.usage?.totalTokens} tokens`)\n  },\n}\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  middleware: [logger],\n})\n```\n\nThe available hooks cover the full lifecycle:\n\n| Hook | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `onConfig` | Transform messages, tools, temperature, system prompts per iteration |\n| `onStructuredOutputConfig` | Transform the structured-output schema/config before the call |\n| `onStart` | Setup tasks (timers, logging) |\n| `onIteration` | Observe each agent-loop iteration |\n| `onChunk` | Transform, expand, or drop individual stream chunks |\n| `onBeforeToolCall` | Intercept tool calls: transform args, skip execution, or abort the run |\n| `onAfterToolCall` | Observe tool results, timing, and errors |\n| `onToolPhaseComplete` | Observe the full batch of tool results for an iteration (e.g. aggregate approval state) |\n| `onUsage` | Track token usage per iteration |\n| `onFinish` / `onAbort` / `onError` | Terminal hooks (exactly one fires per run) |\n\nMiddleware compose naturally. `onConfig` pipes through each middleware in order. `onChunk` pipes chunks through each middleware (if one drops a chunk, later middleware never see it). `onBeforeToolCall` uses first-win semantics: the first middleware that returns a decision short-circuits the rest.\n\nTanStack AI ships several built-in middleware. `toolCacheMiddleware` and `contentGuardMiddleware` come from the `@tanstack/ai/middlewares` subpath, and `otelMiddleware` from `@tanstack/ai/middlewares/otel` (kept on its own subpath so `@opentelemetry/api` stays an optional peer). `toolCacheMiddleware` caches tool results by name and arguments with configurable TTL, LRU eviction, and pluggable storage backends (Redis, localStorage, etc.).\n\n```ts\nimport { toolCacheMiddleware, contentGuardMiddleware } from '@tanstack/ai/middlewares'\nimport { otelMiddleware } from '@tanstack/ai/middlewares/otel'\n```\n\nVercel AI SDK takes a different approach: `wrapLanguageModel()` wraps a model instance with middleware that can intercept and transform calls (and v6 adds `wrapEmbeddingModel()`). It ships several built-in middleware (`extractReasoningMiddleware`, `simulateStreamingMiddleware`, `defaultSettingsMiddleware`, and the new `devToolsMiddleware`), but these all operate at the model level rather than the application level. v6 also exposes per-call options that cover slices of this surface: `experimental_transform` for stream transforms, `experimental_onToolCallStart` / `experimental_onToolCallFinish` callbacks, `prepareStep` for per-step config changes, and `experimental_repairToolCall`. What it doesn't have is a unified middleware system at the application level - named, reusable middleware objects whose hooks span the whole lifecycle, compose in order, and can short-circuit tool calls with first-win semantics.\n\n### No Platform Association\n\nTanStack AI is a pure library. There's no optional platform layer, no gateway abstraction, no hosting-specific features, and no deployment-specific optimizations. Your AI code carries no implicit association with any deployment platform.\n\nThis isn't just philosophical - it means no accidental dependencies on platform-specific features, no gateway abstractions that subtly encourage vendor adoption, and no marketing surface embedded in your technical stack.\n\n### Code Mode\n\nCode Mode lets the model write TypeScript that calls your tools inside a sandbox. One `execute_typescript` call can loop, branch, and `Promise.all` instead of one tool per turn.\n\nTanStack AI ships six isolate drivers behind one `IsolateDriver` interface:\n\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-node`** - Node.js sandbox via `isolated-vm`\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-quickjs`** - QuickJS WASM (browsers and edge)\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-quickjs-bun`** - Native QuickJS on Bun via `bun:ffi`\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-cloudflare`** - Cloudflare Workers\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-daytona`** - Remote Daytona sandbox\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-e2b`** - Remote E2B sandbox\n\nSwap the driver without changing application code. A companion `@tanstack/ai-code-mode-snippets` package gives the model a persistent snippet library. The model can save working TypeScript snippets, list them, and reuse them across sessions. Trust strategies control what gets promoted to a first-class tool.\n\nVercel AI SDK now ships experimental `@ai-sdk/code-mode`. It runs QuickJS only, needs Node 22 or newer, and does not run in the browser or on the edge. Nested tool approvals are rejected. Provider-hosted code execution (Anthropic, xAI, OpenAI) is still a separate path. None of those give the model a persistent, provider-agnostic skill library it builds itself. See [Code Mode](../code-mode/code-mode).\n\n### Coding Agent Sandboxes\n\nSeparately from the JS isolates above, TanStack AI can put a full coding-agent CLI inside an isolated sandbox with a real filesystem, a shell, and a cloned repo. The agent can be Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, or any ACP-compliant agent. Its work streams back through `chat()` like any other run.\n\nA sandboxed run composes three swappable pieces:\n\n- a **provider** (where it runs)\n- a **workspace** (what the agent sees)\n- a **harness adapter** (which agent runs)\n\nThe sandbox is a `chat()` middleware, so the agent's edits and commands arrive as the same AG-UI stream every `useChat` UI already renders.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo, withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'TanStack/ai' }),\n    packageManager: 'pnpm',\n  }),\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  middleware: [withSandbox(sandbox)],\n})\n```\n\nTwo axes are open where the AI SDK's is narrower:\n\n- **Any agent, not a fixed list.** Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode ship as first-party harness packages, and `acpCompatible` (from `@tanstack/ai-acp`) turns *any* [Agent Client Protocol](https://agentclientprotocol.com) agent (`pi`, `gemini --acp`, and [dozens of others](https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/agents)) into a harness by describing how to launch it. Adding an agent does not require a dedicated adapter to exist.\n- **Any sandbox, not one cloud.** The same run executes on `localProcessSandbox` (host dev loop), `dockerSandbox` (container), `sbxSandbox` (Docker Sandboxes microVM), Daytona, Vercel Sandbox, Sprites, or Cloudflare. Swap the provider without touching the harness or workspace. Providers declare their `capabilities()` (`fs`, `exec`, `ports`, `snapshots`, `fork`, `durableFilesystem`, and more) so code degrades across them.\n\nVercel AI SDK 7 added an experimental `HarnessAgent` API for the same idea: run a coding-agent harness in a sandbox and return AI SDK `generate()` / `stream()` results. It ships adapters for Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, Deep Agents, and Pi. The documented path runs them in Vercel Sandbox, with long work on the Workflow SDK. There is no generic ACP escape hatch (each supported harness is its own package), and sandbox support centers on Vercel's own microVM rather than a provider-swappable contract. See [Sandbox providers](../sandbox/providers) and [Harnesses](../sandbox/harnesses).\n\n### Media Generation\n\nTanStack AI provides stable, dedicated APIs for every media generation activity - image, video, speech, transcription, and summarization. Each is a separate, tree-shakeable function with its own adapter per provider.\n\nVercel AI SDK has added several of these capabilities. As of v6, `generateImage()` is stable; video generation is still experimental (`experimental_generateVideo()`); and `generateSpeech()` / `transcribe()` are exported without the `experimental_` prefix but are still documented as experimental features. TanStack AI's media APIs are stable across the board and go further in several areas:\n\n**Image generation** - `generateImage()` with per-model type safety. TypeScript knows that `gpt-image-2` and `dall-e-3` expose different size constraints. Five providers ship adapters: OpenAI (GPT Image, DALL-E), Gemini (Imagen), Grok, OpenRouter, and fal.ai (600+ community models including Flux, SDXL, and more).\n\n```ts\nimport { generateImage } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst result = await generateImage({\n  adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n  prompt: 'A sunset over mountains',\n  size: '1536x1024',\n  numberOfImages: 1,\n})\n```\n\n**Video generation** - `generateVideo()` handles the full async job lifecycle automatically. Video generation APIs are inherently asynchronous - you submit a job, poll for status, and eventually get a result. TanStack AI manages this entire lifecycle with configurable polling intervals and timeouts, streaming status updates back to the client.\n\n```ts\nimport { generateVideo } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiVideo } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst stream = generateVideo({\n  adapter: openaiVideo('sora-2'),\n  prompt: 'A cat playing piano',\n  size: '1280x720',\n  duration: 8,\n  stream: true,          // Stream job lifecycle events\n  pollingInterval: 2000, // Poll every 2 seconds\n})\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  // Receive: job created → status updates → final video URL\n}\n```\n\nVercel AI SDK's `experimental_generateVideo()` returns the video directly without exposing the job lifecycle or streaming status updates.\n\n**Text-to-speech** - `generateSpeech()` supports 6 audio output formats (mp3, opus, aac, flac, wav, pcm), speed control (0.25x to 4x), and five providers: OpenAI (11 voices), Gemini (30+ voices with language hints), Grok, ElevenLabs, and fal.ai.\n\n```ts\nimport { generateSpeech } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiSpeech } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst result = await generateSpeech({\n  adapter: openaiSpeech('tts-1-hd'),\n  text: 'Hello, world!',\n  voice: 'nova',\n  format: 'opus',\n  speed: 1.2,\n})\n```\n\n**Transcription** - `generateTranscription()` supports common output formats (json, text, srt, verbose_json, vtt), word-level timestamps with confidence scores, and four providers (OpenAI, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai), with speaker diarization via OpenAI's `gpt-4o-transcribe-diarize` model.\n\n```ts\nimport { generateTranscription } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiTranscription } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { audioFile } from './audio'\n\nconst result = await generateTranscription({\n  adapter: openaiTranscription('gpt-4o-transcribe'),\n  audio: audioFile,\n  responseFormat: 'verbose_json', // Includes word-level timestamps\n})\n\n// result.words → [{ word: 'Hello', start: 0.0, end: 0.42 }, ...]\n```\n\n**Audio & music generation** - `generateAudio()` generates music and sound effects across Gemini (Lyria), ElevenLabs (music + sound effects), and fal.ai. Vercel AI SDK has no equivalent.\n\n**Summarization** - `summarize()` is a dedicated activity with style control (`bullet-points`, `paragraph`, `concise`), focus topics, and streaming support. Vercel AI SDK has no equivalent - summarization requires calling `generateText()` with a prompt.\n\n**Realtime voice** - `realtimeToken()` enables bidirectional audio streaming with Voice Activity Detection modes (server, semantic, manual), tool calling during voice sessions, and simultaneous audio + text output. Three providers ship realtime adapters: OpenAI (Realtime API), Grok, and ElevenLabs. Vercel AI SDK now ships experimental `experimental_useRealtime` for OpenAI, Google, and xAI (plus AI Gateway). TanStack AI's realtime adapters are stable and include ElevenLabs.\n\nAll media activities follow the same adapter pattern as chat - tree-shakeable imports, per-model type safety, and streaming support. If your app only uses chat, none of this media code enters your bundle.\n\n### Native AG-UI Protocol\n\nThe events TanStack AI streams between server and client are [AG-UI](https://docs.ag-ui.com/) events (`RUN_STARTED`, `TEXT_MESSAGE_*`, `TOOL_CALL_*`, `RUN_FINISHED`), imported directly from `@ag-ui/core` - not a bespoke format with an AG-UI export bolted on. Anything that speaks AG-UI can sit on either side of the wire: AG-UI-compliant agent frameworks behind a TanStack AI frontend, or a TanStack AI client in front of an agent server written in another language entirely.\n\nVercel AI SDK streams its own proprietary UI Message Stream protocol. AG-UI interop requires an external translation layer (`@ag-ui/vercel-ai-sdk`, built and maintained by the AG-UI project), and native support remains an open feature request on the AI SDK repo.\n\n### Hooks for Every Activity\n\nChat isn't the only activity with a hook. Every activity ships one - `useGeneration` (streaming structured output), `useGenerateImage`, `useGenerateAudio`, `useGenerateSpeech`, `useTranscription`, `useSummarize`, `useGenerateVideo`, and `useRealtimeChat` - with the same connection-adapter wiring and devtools integration as `useChat`, across React, Solid, Vue, Svelte, Preact, and Angular.\n\nVercel AI SDK's UI layer has three hooks: `useChat`, `useCompletion`, and `useObject`. Its media functions (`generateImage()`, `experimental_generateVideo()`, speech, transcription) are server-side only - surfacing them in a UI means hand-rolling your own routes and client state.\n\n### Multi-Turn Structured Output\n\nTanStack AI preserves structured output in conversation history instead of leaving it only on a call result. Providers may produce it in the agent loop or through separate finalization; both paths create a typed `StructuredOutputPart`, streamed as a `partial` and completed as a `final`, with the schema generic threading all the way down to `messages[i].parts[j].data`.\n\nVercel AI SDK's structured output (`generateObject` / `streamObject` / `Output`) is per-call: the typed object lives on the call result, the message-part union has no structured-output type, and combining `useChat` with typed structured output means manually parsing model text into custom data parts.\n\n### Debug Logging\n\nSet `debug: true` on any activity and the pipeline prints itself: raw provider chunks, post-middleware output, middleware hook inputs and outputs, tool execution, agent-loop iterations, config transforms, and request metadata - each category individually toggleable, with a pluggable `logger` for structured output. Vercel AI SDK's built-in logging covers provider warnings; richer observability goes through experimental telemetry hooks or the dev-only DevTools recorder rather than a debug log you can flip on anywhere.\n\n### Community Adapter Ecosystem\n\nTanStack AI publishes an open adapter specification. Official LLM adapters:\n\n- OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Vertex, Grok, Groq, OpenRouter, Ollama\n- Bedrock, BytePlus, Mistral, Cohere, ElevenLabs, fal\n- Vercel AI Gateway, and any OpenAI-compatible endpoint via `openaiCompatible`\n\nHarness adapters: Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, and any ACP agent via `acpCompatible`. The community has already built adapters for Decart, Cencori, Cloudflare, Soniox, and Mynth, with a [guide for building your own](../community-adapters/guide).\n\n## Where Vercel AI SDK Excels\n\n**Provider breadth.** Vercel AI SDK ships ~38 first-party, individually typed provider packages, plus a large community list. If you want a specific provider as a dedicated, maintained package without writing an adapter, their coverage is still broader today. Raw model *count* is not the differentiator: TanStack AI's OpenRouter adapter reaches OpenRouter's full catalog, the first-party Vercel Gateway adapter routes with one key, and `openaiCompatible` connects to any OpenAI-compatible endpoint.\n\n**Solid vs Angular.** Both SDKs now ship official Angular. Solid still cuts the other way: AI SDK's Solid package is community-maintained and pinned to an older SDK major, while TanStack AI ships an official, current Solid integration.\n\n**Agent abstraction.** Vercel AI SDK ships a dedicated `Agent` abstraction (the `ToolLoopAgent` class) that packages a model, tools, instructions, and loop settings into a reusable object with `.generate()` and `.stream()` methods, plus `InferAgentUIMessage` for end-to-end type safety. TanStack AI composes these pieces per call rather than offering a single agent class.\n\n**AI Gateway.** Vercel's optional AI Gateway adds centralized provider management: failover routing, caching, and a single key across providers, integrated with the Vercel platform (and used by default when no provider is configured). TanStack AI ships no gateway of its own. For the same centralized routing, use the first-class [OpenRouter](../adapters/openrouter) adapter or the [Vercel AI Gateway](../adapters/vercel-gateway) adapter. Neither attaches a platform layer to the rest of your stack.\n\n**React Server Components.** Vercel AI SDK has an RSC integration via `@ai-sdk/rsc` (`AIState`, `StreamableValue`, `streamUI`). Note that Vercel documents this as experimental and recommends AI SDK UI for production - so it's an option for Next.js RSC apps rather than the primary path.\n\n## Side-by-Side: Key Differences\n\n### Tool Definition\n\n**TanStack AI** - Isomorphic definitions with separate runtime implementations:\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { weatherApi } from './weather'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Get current weather for a location',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temp: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n})\n\n// Server implementation\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeather.server(async ({ city }) => {\n  const data = await weatherApi.get(city)\n  return { temp: data.temperature, condition: data.condition }\n})\n\n// Client implementation\nconst getWeatherClient = getWeather.client(async ({ city }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/weather?city=${city}`)\n  return res.json()\n})\n```\n\n**Vercel AI SDK** - Tool objects via the `tool()` helper:\n\n```ts\nimport { generateText, tool } from 'ai'\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { weatherApi } from './weather'\n\nconst result = await generateText({\n  model: openai('gpt-5.5'),\n  tools: {\n    getWeather: tool({\n      description: 'Get current weather for a location',\n      inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n      execute: async ({ city }) => {\n        const data = await weatherApi.get(city)\n        return { temp: data.temperature, condition: data.condition }\n      },\n    }),\n  },\n  prompt: \"What's the weather in Tokyo?\",\n})\n```\n\nThe TanStack approach separates the tool contract from its implementation, making tools reusable across server and client contexts.\n\n### Persistence\n\n**TanStack AI** - Middleware writes the transcript. The client reloads it.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  threadId: 'support-chat',\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  persistence: true,\n})\n```\n\n**Vercel AI SDK** - You own `saveChat` / `loadChat` and call them from `onEnd`:\n\n```ts\nimport { convertToModelMessages, createUIMessageStreamResponse, streamText, toUIMessageStream } from 'ai'\nimport { loadChat, saveChat } from './chat-store'\n\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { message, id } = await req.json()\n  const messages = [...(await loadChat(id)), message]\n\n  const result = streamText({\n    model: 'openai/gpt-5.5',\n    messages: await convertToModelMessages(messages),\n  })\n\n  return createUIMessageStreamResponse({\n    stream: toUIMessageStream({\n      stream: result.stream,\n      originalMessages: messages,\n      onEnd: ({ messages: next }) => {\n        saveChat({ chatId: id, messages: next })\n      },\n    }),\n  })\n}\n```\n\nTanStack AI also persists run status and pending approvals, and it can restore a long media generation into the same hook fields. Vercel AI SDK leaves that wiring to you. Resume of a live stream is a separate layer on both sides: TanStack AI uses `StreamDurability`, Vercel AI SDK uses Redis plus `resumable-stream`.\n\n### Agent Loop Control\n\n**TanStack AI** - Composable strategies:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, combineStrategies, maxIterations, untilFinishReason } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\nimport { estimatedTokens } from './cost'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Help me plan a trip.' }]\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools,\n  agentLoopStrategy: combineStrategies([\n    maxIterations(10),\n    untilFinishReason(['stop']),\n    ({ iterationCount }) => estimatedTokens(iterationCount) < 50_000,\n  ]),\n})\n```\n\n**Vercel AI SDK** - `stopWhen` conditions (v5+):\n\n```ts\nimport { generateText, stepCountIs } from 'ai'\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\n\nconst result = await generateText({\n  model: openai('gpt-5.5'),\n  tools,\n  stopWhen: stepCountIs(10), // also: hasToolCall('name'), or a custom function\n  prompt: 'Help me plan a trip.',\n})\n```\n\nBoth let you compose multiple stopping conditions - `stopWhen` accepts an array of conditions including custom functions, and v6 adds a reusable `Agent` class. The remaining nuance is ergonomic: TanStack AI's strategies are plain `(state) => boolean` predicates combined with `combineStrategies`, so token budgets and custom business logic are first-class without reaching for a built-in condition.\n\n### Tree-Shaking\n\n**TanStack AI** - Separate adapters per activity:\n\n```ts\n// Only bundles chat + OpenAI text adapter\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n```\n\n**Vercel AI SDK** - Single provider import:\n\n```ts\n// Provider package includes all model types\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'\n```\n\nIn TanStack AI, each activity (chat, image, speech, video, transcription, summarization) is a separate adapter function. You import `openaiText` for chat and `openaiImage` for image generation - they're independent modules. Vercel AI SDK's provider packages are more monolithic.\n\n## When to Choose TanStack AI\n\n- **Bundle size matters** - Tree-shakeable adapters per activity mean smaller bundles\n- **AG-UI native** - The wire protocol is AG-UI end to end; interoperate with the agent-UI ecosystem and non-TypeScript agent servers without a translation layer\n- **Solid, Preact, Angular, or React Native** - One headless core covers React, Solid, Vue, Svelte, Preact, Angular, and React Native (via XHR adapters), all officially maintained\n- **Hooks beyond chat** - `useGeneration`, `useGenerateImage`, `useSummarize`, and the rest of the generation hook family across every supported framework\n- **Isomorphic tools** - Define a tool once and derive `.server()` / `.client()` implementations from one contract\n- **App-level middleware** - Lifecycle hooks for chunks, tool calls, usage, and errors - not just model wrapping\n- **Chat and generation persistence** - `withPersistence` plus `reconstructChat`, or a browser store; long media runs restore through `withGenerationPersistence`\n- **Resumable streams** - A durability adapter on the response; `useChat` reconnects without Redis\n- **Durable sandbox runs** - Detach, journal, takeover, and reaper so a coding agent survives a closed tab\n- **Realtime voice** - Stable bidirectional audio across OpenAI, Grok, and ElevenLabs\n- **No vendor association** - Pure library with no platform layer\n- **Per-model type safety** - TypeScript narrows options per model, not per provider\n- **Code Mode** - Five isolate drivers (Node, QuickJS WASM, QuickJS Bun, Cloudflare, Daytona) plus an LLM-writable skill library\n- **Coding agent sandboxes** - Run Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, or any ACP agent in a swappable sandbox (local, Docker, Docker Sandboxes, Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Cloudflare), streamed through `chat()`\n- **Flexible transport** - SSE, HTTP streams, XHR, RPC, direct iterables, or custom adapters\n- **MCP, two ways** - A standalone host-side client (`@tanstack/ai-mcp`) with pools, codegen, and managed `chat()` lifecycle, plus a provider-routed `mcpTool()`\n\n## When to Choose Vercel AI SDK\n\n- **Need a first-party package for a specific provider** - ~38 dedicated, individually typed provider packages today (TanStack reaches comparable model breadth via OpenRouter, Vercel Gateway, and `openaiCompatible`)\n- **Agent abstraction** - A reusable `Agent` (`ToolLoopAgent`) class with end-to-end UI message types\n- **Vercel platform** - AI Gateway, observability, and deployment optimization\n- **React Server Components** - RSC primitives via `@ai-sdk/rsc` (experimental; AI SDK UI is the recommended production path)\n- **Workflow-backed long runs on Vercel** - experimental `HarnessAgent` plus the Workflow SDK, if your stack is already that platform\n\n## Getting Started\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-react @tanstack/ai-openai\nvue: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-vue @tanstack/ai-openai\nsolid: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-solid @tanstack/ai-openai\nsvelte: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-svelte @tanstack/ai-openai\npreact: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-preact @tanstack/ai-openai\nangular: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-angular @tanstack/ai-openai\nvanilla: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-client @tanstack/ai-openai\noctane: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-octane @tanstack/ai-openai octane\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\nSee the [Quick Start Guide](../getting-started/quick-start) to build your first chat application, or explore the [full documentation](../getting-started/overview).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: TanStack AI vs Vercel AI SDK\nid: vercel-ai-sdk\norder: 1\ndescription: \"How TanStack AI compares to the Vercel AI SDK: persistence, resumable streams, durable sandbox runs, code mode, harnesses, and LLM adapters.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - vercel ai sdk\n  - comparison\n  - ai sdk\n  - alternatives\n  - typescript ai sdk\n  - tool calling\n  - llm\n  - persistence\n  - resumable streams\n  - durable runs\n  - code mode\n  - sandbox\n---\n\nBoth TanStack AI and Vercel AI SDK are open-source TypeScript toolkits for building AI-powered applications. They share common ground - streaming chat, tool calling, multi-provider support, and deploy-anywhere flexibility - but they approach the problem from fundamentally different directions.\n\nTanStack AI treats AI as a **library composition problem**. Every piece - adapters, tools, agent loops, transport, UI - is a composable building block. You import what you need, compose it how you want, and ship it wherever you want. No platform layer, no gateway abstraction, no implicit associations.\n\nVercel AI SDK treats AI as a **full-stack platform problem**. It provides a broad surface area of primitives with optional platform integration for gateway routing, observability, and deployment optimization.\n\nThis article compares the two SDKs from TanStack AI's perspective, with honest acknowledgment of where each excels.\n\n## Feature Comparison\n\nVersions referenced below: TanStack AI as of this writing; Vercel AI SDK `ai@7.x` (v7 is current).\n\n| Feature | TanStack AI | Vercel AI SDK |\n|---------|------------|---------------|\n| License | MIT | Apache 2.0 |\n| Hosting | Works anywhere | Works anywhere |\n| Providers | 16 official LLM adapters (OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Vertex, Grok, Groq, OpenRouter, Ollama, Bedrock, BytePlus, Mistral, Cohere, ElevenLabs, fal, Vercel Gateway, `openaiCompatible`) plus 5 harness adapters; community adapters for more | ~38 first-party provider packages (plus community); 100+ models via AI Gateway |\n| Framework Hooks | React, Solid, Svelte, Vue, Preact, Angular (+ React Native) | React, Vue, Svelte, Angular (Solid is community-maintained) |\n| Generation UI Hooks | One hook per activity: chat, structured output, image, audio, speech, transcription, summarize, video, realtime | `useChat`, `useCompletion`, `useObject` |\n| Wire Protocol | Native AG-UI events end to end | Proprietary UI Message Stream; AG-UI via external translation layer |\n| Streaming | Built-in with configurable chunk strategies | Built-in with progressive delivery |\n| Chat Persistence | `withPersistence` middleware + `reconstructChat`; server store or browser stores (`localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, IndexedDB) | Guide: you own `saveChat` / `loadChat` / `validateUIMessages` |\n| Generation Persistence | `withGenerationPersistence` restores long media runs into the same hook fields | - |\n| Resumable Streams | `StreamDurability` in core; `memoryStream` or `durableStream`; `useChat` reconnects on its own | Redis + `resumable-stream` + `activeStreamId`; you wire POST and GET |\n| Durable Sandbox Runs | Detach, journal, takeover, reaper; the agent keeps working after the tab closes | `HarnessAgent` + Workflow SDK (experimental); centered on Vercel Sandbox |\n| Tool Calling | Isomorphic `.server()` / `.client()` system | `tool()` objects; client execution via `onToolCall` |\n| Agent Loop Control | Composable strategy functions `(state) => boolean` | `stopWhen` conditions + `Agent` (`ToolLoopAgent`) class |\n| Tool Approval | Per-tool `needsApproval` with batched approval flow | Per-tool `needsApproval` (human-in-the-loop) |\n| Type Safety | Per-model type narrowing | Per-provider types |\n| Tree-Shaking | Separate adapter per activity (text, image, speech, etc.) | Monolithic provider packages |\n| Lazy Tool Discovery | Built-in - works across every provider | Anthropic-only tool search with `deferLoading` (provider-hosted) |\n| Connection Adapters | SSE, HTTP stream, XHR (SSE/stream), RPC, direct async iterables, `fetcher`, custom | SSE-based data stream protocol (`ChatTransport`) |\n| Middleware | App-level lifecycle hooks (config, iterations, chunks, tool calls, usage, errors) | Model-level wrapping via `wrapLanguageModel()` |\n| Extend Adapter | Add custom/fine-tuned models with per-model type narrowing | `customProvider()` / `createProviderRegistry()` (string model ids) |\n| Structured Outputs | Typed `StructuredOutputPart`, streamed alongside tools and preserved per turn in message history | `generateObject()` / `streamObject()` / `Output` API (per-call; no structured-output message part) |\n| Image Generation | Stable API with per-model type safety (OpenAI, Gemini, Grok, OpenRouter, fal.ai) | `generateImage()` (stable) |\n| Video Generation | Stable API with async job lifecycle (OpenAI, fal.ai) | `experimental_generateVideo()` |\n| Text-to-Speech | Stable API, 6 output formats, speed control (OpenAI, Gemini, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai) | `generateSpeech()` (experimental) |\n| Transcription | Stable API with word timestamps and diarization (OpenAI, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai) | `transcribe()` (experimental) |\n| Audio / Music Generation | `generateAudio()` for music & sound effects (Gemini, ElevenLabs, fal.ai) | - |\n| Summarization | Dedicated `summarize()` with streaming and style options | - |\n| Code Mode | 5 isolate drivers you run yourself: Node `isolated-vm`, QuickJS WASM, QuickJS Bun, Cloudflare, Daytona | Experimental `@ai-sdk/code-mode` (QuickJS only, Node 22+, not browser or edge); also provider-hosted code-execution tools |\n| Code Mode Skills | LLM-writable persistent skill library | Static skill uploads; no model-built library |\n| Coding Agent Sandboxes | First-party Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode + any ACP agent via `acpCompatible`; local-process, Docker, Docker Sandboxes (`sbx`), Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Cloudflare | `HarnessAgent` (experimental): Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, Deep Agents, Pi; centered on Vercel Sandbox |\n| Realtime Voice | Stable: OpenAI, Grok, and ElevenLabs with VAD modes and tool support | Experimental `experimental_useRealtime` (OpenAI, Google, xAI + Gateway) |\n| DevTools | Isomorphic in-app panel via TanStack DevTools (all frameworks, media previews) | `devToolsMiddleware` + local inspector (server-side, dev-only) |\n| Debug Logging | One flag, per-category toggles, pluggable logger | Warning logs + experimental telemetry hooks |\n| MCP Client | Standalone host-side client (`@tanstack/ai-mcp`) + provider-routed `mcpTool()` | Built-in (`@ai-sdk/mcp`, stable) |\n| MCP Apps (Interactive Widgets) | `ui://` widgets via `@mcp-ui/client`; React + Preact + framework-agnostic bridge; multi-server routing, pluggable session store, link-scheme hardening | `experimental_MCPAppRenderer` (React only); model-vs-app tool split; iframe sandbox + allowlist |\n| Platform Association | None - pure library | Optional Vercel integration |\n\n## Where TanStack AI Excels\n\n### Per-Model Type Safety\n\nWhen you select a provider and model, TypeScript narrows the exact options, capabilities, and input modalities available for that specific model - not a union of everything the provider supports.\n\nEach provider adapter contains a comprehensive `model-meta.ts` that maps every model to its capabilities: supported input modalities, context windows, and provider-specific options. When you write `openaiText('gpt-5.5')`, the type system knows exactly what that model can do.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\n// TypeScript knows gpt-5.5 supports text + image input\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{\n    role: 'user',\n    content: [\n      { type: 'text', content: 'What is in this image?' },\n      { type: 'image', source: { type: 'url', value: 'https://example.com/photo.jpg' } },\n    ],\n  }],\n})\n```\n\nIf you pass an image content part to a text-only model, TypeScript catches it at compile time.\n\n### Tree-Shakeable Adapters\n\nEvery AI activity - chat, summarization, image generation, speech, transcription, video - is a separate import. Every provider exposes separate adapter functions per activity. If your app only uses chat, image generation code never enters your bundle.\n\n```ts ignore\n// Only chat code is bundled - nothing else\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\n// vs. importing activities you actually need\nimport { chat, generateImage } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText, openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n```\n\nThis is architectural, not incidental. Each adapter implements a specific interface (`TextAdapter`, `ImageAdapter`, `TTSAdapter`, etc.) and lives in its own module. Modern bundlers eliminate everything you don't import.\n\n### Isomorphic Tools\n\n`toolDefinition()` creates a shared contract - name, description, input schema, output schema - that can be implemented for different runtimes. `.server()` adds a server-side implementation with access to databases and APIs. `.client()` adds a client-side implementation that runs in the browser.\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { db } from './db'\n\n// Define once - shared validation contract\nconst addToCartDef = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'addToCart',\n  description: 'Add an item to the shopping cart',\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    itemId: z.string(),\n    quantity: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({\n    success: z.boolean(),\n    cartId: z.string(),\n  }),\n})\n\n// Server implementation - database access\nconst addToCartServer = addToCartDef.server(async ({ itemId, quantity }) => {\n  const cart = await db.carts.addItem(itemId, quantity)\n  return { success: true, cartId: cart.id }\n})\n\n// Client implementation - runs in the browser\nconst addToCartClient = addToCartDef.client(async ({ itemId, quantity }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/cart`, {\n    method: 'POST',\n    body: JSON.stringify({ itemId, quantity }),\n  })\n  return res.json()\n})\n```\n\nThe same schema validates inputs and outputs on both sides. The type system tracks whether a tool is a `ServerTool` or `ClientTool` at compile time.\n\nVercel AI SDK defines tools with a `tool()` helper and does support client-side execution - a tool with no `execute` function is handled in the browser via the UI hook's `onToolCall` callback, with the result returned through `addToolOutput` (renamed from `addToolResult` in v6). What it doesn't have is a single shared contract that produces separate `.server()` and `.client()` implementations: server and client tool code are declared independently rather than derived from one definition.\n\n### Composable Agent Loop Strategies\n\nTanStack AI provides agent loop control as composable pure functions. Each strategy is `(state) => boolean` - return `true` to continue, `false` to stop.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, maxIterations, untilFinishReason, combineStrategies } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Help me plan a trip.' }]\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools,\n  agentLoopStrategy: combineStrategies([\n    maxIterations(10),\n    untilFinishReason(['stop', 'length']),\n  ]),\n})\n```\n\n`combineStrategies` composes them with AND logic - all strategies must agree to continue. You can add custom strategies alongside built-in ones:\n\n```ts\nimport { maxIterations, untilFinishReason, combineStrategies } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { estimatedCost, budget } from './cost'\n\ncombineStrategies([\n  maxIterations(10),\n  untilFinishReason(['stop']),\n  // Custom: stop if budget exceeded\n  ({ iterationCount }) => estimatedCost(iterationCount) < budget,\n])\n```\n\nVercel AI SDK (v5+) controls agent loops via `stopWhen`, which accepts composable stopping conditions like `stepCountIs(n)` and `hasToolCall(name)` (the default is `stepCountIs(20)`), and v6 adds a dedicated `Agent` abstraction (the `ToolLoopAgent` class) that bundles model, tools, instructions, and loop settings into a reusable object. The remaining difference is in the composition model: TanStack AI's strategies are arbitrary `(state) => boolean` predicates you write inline and combine with `combineStrategies`, so a stopping condition can encode any business logic (token budgets, cost ceilings, custom state checks) without waiting for a built-in condition to exist. Vercel's `stopWhen` also accepts custom functions, so the gap here is smaller than it once was.\n\n### Lazy Tool Discovery\n\nWhen your application has dozens of tools, sending all their schemas to the LLM on every request wastes tokens. TanStack AI solves this with lazy tool discovery.\n\nMark tools as `lazy: true` and they won't be sent to the LLM initially. Instead, a synthetic discovery tool is injected that lets the LLM request tool schemas on demand:\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst searchProducts = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'searchProducts',\n  description: 'Search the product catalog',\n  lazy: true, // Not sent to LLM initially\n  inputSchema: z.object({ query: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.array(z.object({ id: z.string(), name: z.string() })),\n})\n```\n\nThe LLM sees a lightweight `__lazy__tool__discovery__` tool listing available tool names. When it needs one, it calls the discovery tool to get the full schema, then uses the real tool. For applications with large tool inventories, this significantly reduces per-request token costs.\n\nVercel AI SDK 6 added a provider-specific analogue for Anthropic: the tool search provider tool (`toolSearchBm25` / `toolSearchRegex`) with per-tool `deferLoading`, where deferred tools are excluded from the initial prompt and discovered on demand. It's Anthropic-only and runs provider-side; TanStack AI's lazy discovery works across every provider and runs in your own agent loop.\n\n### Model Context Protocol (MCP)\n\nTanStack AI connects to MCP servers two ways, and you can mix them in a single `chat()` run:\n\n- **Host-side client** (`@tanstack/ai-mcp`) - your server connects directly to any MCP server. `createMCPClient` (single server) and `createMCPClients` (multi-server pool) discover and execute tools, read resources, and fetch prompts over Streamable HTTP, SSE, or stdio transports, with OAuth 2.1 (`authProvider`) and static-token auth.\n- **Provider-routed** (`mcpTool()`) - the *provider* connects to the MCP server on your behalf (OpenAI Responses API, Anthropic), so no MCP traffic flows through your server at all.\n\nThe host-side client goes beyond basic discovery:\n\n- **Managed lifecycle** - hand clients to `chat()` via the `mcp` option and it discovers tools and closes connections when the run ends - no `try/finally` per route.\n- **Multi-server pools** - `createMCPClients` connects to many servers in parallel, auto-prefixing each server's tools to prevent name collisions.\n- **Three modes of type safety** - untyped auto-discovery, `toolDefinition()`-typed allowlists with Zod validation, or fully generated per-server types via the `tanstack-ai-mcp` CLI.\n- **Lazy discovery** - `tools({ lazy: true })` defers sending tool schemas to the LLM, plugging into TanStack AI's lazy tool discovery to cut token usage on tool-heavy servers.\n- **Resources & prompts** - inject MCP resources and prompts into a run with `mcpResourceToContentPart` and `mcpPromptToMessages`.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { createMCPClient } from '@tanstack/ai-mcp'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'What tools are available?' }]\n\nconst mcp = await createMCPClient({\n  transport: { type: 'http', url: 'https://my-mcp-server.example.com/mcp' },\n})\n\n// chat() discovers the tools and closes the client when the run ends\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  mcp: { clients: [mcp] },\n})\n```\n\n`createMCPClient` tries protocol `2026-07-28` first. If the server does not support that protocol, the client uses the 2025 initialize handshake.\n\n- A client app imports `@modelcontextprotocol/client`.\n- A server app imports `@modelcontextprotocol/server`.\n\nPackage names for an `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` import are in [MCP SDK packages](../migration/mcp-sdk).\n\nVercel AI SDK's `@ai-sdk/mcp` (`createMCPClient`) is a stable host-side client with HTTP/SSE transports, OAuth, resource reading, and prompt templates. TanStack AI's `@tanstack/ai-mcp` matches that surface and adds generated end-to-end types, multi-server pools, lazy discovery, a managed `chat()` lifecycle, and the provider-routed `mcpTool()` alternative.\n\n### MCP Apps (Interactive Widgets)\n\nBoth SDKs implement [MCP Apps](https://modelcontextprotocol.io) — the ratified MCP extension (standardized 2026-01-26) where a server returns a `ui://` resource so a tool result renders as an interactive widget in a sandboxed iframe instead of raw JSON. Both keep the widget HTML out of model input and render it in a sandboxed iframe with a tool allowlist and safe link handling. TanStack AI's implementation is more built out along three axes:\n\n- **More than one framework.** Widgets render via `@tanstack/ai-react/mcp-apps` and `@tanstack/ai-preact/mcp-apps`, and the bridge that routes widget actions (`createMcpAppBridge`) lives in the framework-agnostic `@tanstack/ai-client`, so a new framework only needs a thin renderer. Vercel's `experimental_MCPAppRenderer` and its bridge live in `@ai-sdk/react` — React only.\n- **Multi-server routing.** Each `UIResourcePart` carries a `serverId` (the pool prefix from `createMCPClients`), and interactive calls route back to the exact server that produced the widget — automatically when you run a multi-server pool. The call handler also enforces an unconditional same-server exposure check (`toolName` must be a tool that server actually exposes) with an optional `allowTool` restriction AND-ed on top.\n- **Session persistence & serverless-safety.** The call handler reconnects per call from a transport descriptor (stateless, serverless-safe by default), and stateful transports opt into a pluggable `McpSessionStore` — an `inMemoryMcpSessionStore` ships, and SQL/KV backends drop in behind the same interface.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useChat, useMcpAppBridge } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents } from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { MCPAppResource } from '@tanstack/ai-react/mcp-apps'\n\nexport function Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  })\n\n  // Routes widget tool-calls to /api/mcp-apps/call by serverId; only http/https/mailto links pass through.\n  const bridge = useMcpAppBridge({\n    threadId: 'weather-chat',\n    callEndpoint: '/api/mcp-apps/call',\n    chat: { sendMessage: async (content) => void sendMessage({ content }) },\n    onLink: (url) => window.open(url, '_blank', 'noopener'),\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {messages.map((m) =>\n        m.parts.map((part, i) =>\n          part.type === 'ui-resource' ? (\n            <MCPAppResource\n              key={i}\n              part={part}\n              bridge={bridge}\n              sandbox={{ url: new URL('https://your-app.example.com/mcp-sandbox.html') }}\n            />\n          ) : null,\n        ),\n      )}\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nVercel AI SDK 7 covers the core flow and adds one thing TanStack AI doesn't: `splitMCPAppTools`, which separates *model-visible* tools from *app-only* tools the widget can call but the model never sees. Both implementations are new — Vercel marks its renderer `experimental_`, and TanStack AI's writeback of widget tool-calls into chat history is still out of scope. See the [MCP Apps guide](../mcp/apps) for the full API.\n\n### Headless Client Architecture\n\n`ChatClient` is a framework-agnostic class that manages the entire chat lifecycle - streaming, message state, tool execution, approval flows, and connection management. Every framework integration wraps this single client:\n\n- `@tanstack/ai-react` - `useChat` hook wraps `ChatClient`\n- `@tanstack/ai-solid` - `useChat` hook wraps `ChatClient`\n- `@tanstack/ai-vue` - `useChat` composable wraps `ChatClient`\n- `@tanstack/ai-svelte` - `createChat` wraps `ChatClient` (Svelte 5 runes)\n- `@tanstack/ai-preact` - `useChat` hook wraps `ChatClient`\n- `@tanstack/ai-angular` - `injectChat` wraps `ChatClient`\n\nNo framework-specific logic in the core. If a new framework emerges, it only needs a thin reactive wrapper.\n\n`ChatClient` also accepts a persistence adapter (`ChatClientPersistence`) for saving and restoring conversations client-side, and a typed runtime `context` that flows through to tools and middleware.\n\n### Persistence\n\nA reload or a second device must not wipe the conversation. TanStack AI treats that as a first-class layer, not a guide you implement by hand.\n\n`withPersistence` writes the transcript, run status, and pending approvals into your store. The client has two modes:\n\n- **`persistence: true`** puts the server in charge. The browser caches nothing and asks the server for the thread on mount.\n- **`persistence: <adapter>`** puts the browser in charge, with `localStoragePersistence()`, `sessionStoragePersistence()`, or `indexedDBPersistence()`.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nfunction Chat() {\n  const { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'support-chat',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n  return <button onClick={() => sendMessage('hi')}>{messages.length}</button>\n}\n```\n\nWith `persistence: true`, add a `GET` that calls `reconstructChat`. That handler returns the stored transcript. If you also pass a durability adapter, the same `GET` can resume a run that is still streaming. See [Persistence](../persistence/overview).\n\nVercel AI SDK documents message persistence as a pattern. You write `saveChat`, `loadChat`, and `validateUIMessages`. There is no middleware that owns the transcript for you.\n\nLong media runs (video, batch images) use a second middleware, `withGenerationPersistence`. A reload restores `status`, `result`, and `error` into the same generation hook. See [Generation Persistence](../persistence/generation-persistence).\n\n### Resumable Streams\n\nA dropped socket is a different problem from a missing transcript. The model already ran. You want the rest of the tokens, not a second bill.\n\nPass a durability adapter to `toServerSentEventsResponse`. The adapter records every chunk before delivery. On reconnect the client sends the last offset and the server replays the log.\n\n- **`memoryStream`** from `@tanstack/ai` keeps the log in process memory. Use it in development.\n- **`durableStream`** from `@tanstack/ai-durable-stream` writes to an external [Durable Streams](https://durablestreams.com) backend. Use it in production.\n\n`useChat` reconnects on its own. You add a `GET` that calls `resumeServerSentEventsResponse`.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n  })\n}\n\nexport function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) })\n}\n```\n\nIn production, swap `memoryStream(request)` for `durableStream(request, options)`. Everything else stays the same. See [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview).\n\nVercel AI SDK resume needs Redis, the `resumable-stream` package, an `activeStreamId` you store, and two routes you write. The SDK gives `resume` on `useChat` and a `consumeSseStream` callback. The store and the wiring stay yours.\n\n### Durable Sandbox Runs\n\nA coding agent can run for ten minutes. The tab will close. The wifi will drop. The next request can land on a different host.\n\nWithout durability, TanStack AI destroys the sandbox on disconnect. That stops a runaway bill. With durability, the agent detaches, writes a journal at `/tmp/tanstack-runs/<runId>.ndjson`, and a later `GET` can take the run over.\n\nTurn it on with both `runs` and `durability` on `withSandbox`. Passing only one leaves the default destroy-on-disconnect behavior.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { withLocks } from '@tanstack/ai/locks'\nimport { claudeCodeText } from '@tanstack/ai-claude-code'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { locks } from './locks'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\nimport { sandbox } from './sandbox'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const adapter = memoryStream(request)\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: claudeCodeText('claude-opus-4-8'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n    middleware: [\n      withPersistence(persistence),\n      withLocks(locks),\n      withSandbox(sandbox, {\n        runs: persistence.stores.runs,\n        durability: { adapter },\n      }),\n    ],\n  })\n\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, { durability: { adapter } })\n}\n```\n\nSchedule the reaper, or sandboxes keep billing after no reader is left. Use a distributed lock across hosts. See [Durable Runs](../sandbox/durable-runs).\n\nVercel AI SDK runs long harness work through experimental `HarnessAgent` and the Workflow SDK, centered on Vercel Sandbox. TanStack AI uses the same `chat()` + `withSandbox` path on every provider: local process, Docker, Docker Sandboxes, Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, or Cloudflare.\n\n### Connection Adapters\n\nTanStack AI ships six built-in connection adapters plus a custom adapter interface:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  fetchServerSentEvents,\n  fetchHttpStream,\n  xhrServerSentEvents,\n  xhrHttpStream,\n  stream,\n  rpcStream,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-client'\nimport { chatOnServer } from './server'\nimport { api } from './api'\n\n// Server-Sent Events (standard)\nfetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\n\n// Raw HTTP streaming (newline-delimited JSON)\nfetchHttpStream('/api/chat')\n\n// XHR-based SSE / HTTP streaming (React Native / Expo, where fetch streaming is unavailable)\nxhrServerSentEvents('/api/chat')\nxhrHttpStream('/api/chat')\n\n// Direct async iterables (TanStack Start server functions)\nstream((messages) => chatOnServer({ messages }))\n\n// RPC-based transport\nrpcStream((messages, data) => api.streamResponse(messages, data))\n\n// Or implement your own ConnectionAdapter\n```\n\nEach adapter accepts static or dynamic (function-based) URLs and options. There's also a lighter-weight `fetcher` option on `ChatClient` / `useChat` for wiring a server function directly without a full adapter. Swap transport without changing application code. Vercel AI SDK centers on its SSE-based data stream protocol and a `ChatTransport` interface for extensibility, but doesn't ship the same breadth of built-in adapters - notably the XHR variants for React Native.\n\n### Extend Adapter\n\nWhen you use fine-tuned models, OpenAI-compatible proxies, or custom model endpoints, `extendAdapter()` lets you add them to any provider adapter with full type safety:\n\n```ts\nimport { extendAdapter, createModel } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst customModels = [\n  createModel('my-fine-tuned-gpt4', ['text', 'image']),\n  createModel('company-internal-llm', ['text']),\n] as const\n\nconst myOpenai = extendAdapter(openaiText, customModels)\n\n// Full autocomplete - original models + custom models\nconst adapter = myOpenai('my-fine-tuned-gpt4')\n```\n\nYour custom models appear in autocomplete alongside official ones. Vercel AI SDK covers the registration half of this with the now-stable `customProvider()` (custom and aliased model ids, settings overrides) and `createProviderRegistry()`; the difference is type-safety depth - registry model ids are plain strings, while `extendAdapter()` gives custom models the same literal-type narrowing and per-model option gating as official ones.\n\n### Middleware\n\nTanStack AI's middleware system hooks into every stage of the `chat()` lifecycle: configuration, streaming, tool execution, usage tracking, and completion. Each middleware is a plain object with named hooks that fire at specific phases.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, EventType, type ChatMiddleware, type StreamChunk } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Hello' }]\n\nconst logger: ChatMiddleware = {\n  name: 'logger',\n  onStart: (ctx) => {\n    console.log(`[${ctx.requestId}] Chat started`)\n  },\n  onChunk: (ctx, chunk) => {\n    // Transform, expand, or drop chunks. `type` is the discriminant, so it\n    // narrows `chunk` to the matching event and types `delta` as `string`.\n    if (chunk.type === EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT) {\n      return { ...chunk, delta: chunk.delta.replace(/\\b\\d{3}-\\d{2}-\\d{4}\\b/g, '[REDACTED]') }\n    }\n  },\n  onBeforeToolCall: (ctx, hookCtx) => {\n    // Intercept tool calls: transform args, skip, or abort\n    if (hookCtx.toolName === 'deleteDatabase') {\n      return { type: 'abort', reason: 'Dangerous operation blocked' }\n    }\n  },\n  onAfterToolCall: (ctx, info) => {\n    console.log(`${info.toolName}: ${info.ok ? 'success' : 'failed'} in ${info.duration}ms`)\n  },\n  onFinish: (ctx, info) => {\n    console.log(`Done in ${info.duration}ms, ${info.usage?.totalTokens} tokens`)\n  },\n}\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  middleware: [logger],\n})\n```\n\nThe available hooks cover the full lifecycle:\n\n| Hook | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `onConfig` | Transform messages, tools, temperature, system prompts per iteration |\n| `onStructuredOutputConfig` | Transform the structured-output schema/config before the call |\n| `onStart` | Setup tasks (timers, logging) |\n| `onIteration` | Observe each agent-loop iteration |\n| `onChunk` | Transform, expand, or drop individual stream chunks |\n| `onBeforeToolCall` | Intercept tool calls: transform args, skip execution, or abort the run |\n| `onAfterToolCall` | Observe tool results, timing, and errors |\n| `onToolPhaseComplete` | Observe the full batch of tool results for an iteration (e.g. aggregate approval state) |\n| `onUsage` | Track token usage per iteration |\n| `onFinish` / `onAbort` / `onError` | Terminal hooks (exactly one fires per run) |\n\nMiddleware compose naturally. `onConfig` pipes through each middleware in order. `onChunk` pipes chunks through each middleware (if one drops a chunk, later middleware never see it). `onBeforeToolCall` uses first-win semantics: the first middleware that returns a decision short-circuits the rest.\n\nTanStack AI ships several built-in middleware. `toolCacheMiddleware` and `contentGuardMiddleware` come from the `@tanstack/ai/middlewares` subpath, and `otelMiddleware` from `@tanstack/ai/middlewares/otel` (kept on its own subpath so `@opentelemetry/api` stays an optional peer). `toolCacheMiddleware` caches tool results by name and arguments with configurable TTL, LRU eviction, and pluggable storage backends (Redis, localStorage, etc.).\n\n```ts\nimport { toolCacheMiddleware, contentGuardMiddleware } from '@tanstack/ai/middlewares'\nimport { otelMiddleware } from '@tanstack/ai/middlewares/otel'\n```\n\nVercel AI SDK takes a different approach: `wrapLanguageModel()` wraps a model instance with middleware that can intercept and transform calls (and v6 adds `wrapEmbeddingModel()`). It ships several built-in middleware (`extractReasoningMiddleware`, `simulateStreamingMiddleware`, `defaultSettingsMiddleware`, and the new `devToolsMiddleware`), but these all operate at the model level rather than the application level. v6 also exposes per-call options that cover slices of this surface: `experimental_transform` for stream transforms, `experimental_onToolCallStart` / `experimental_onToolCallFinish` callbacks, `prepareStep` for per-step config changes, and `experimental_repairToolCall`. What it doesn't have is a unified middleware system at the application level - named, reusable middleware objects whose hooks span the whole lifecycle, compose in order, and can short-circuit tool calls with first-win semantics.\n\n### No Platform Association\n\nTanStack AI is a pure library. There's no optional platform layer, no gateway abstraction, no hosting-specific features, and no deployment-specific optimizations. Your AI code carries no implicit association with any deployment platform.\n\nThis isn't just philosophical - it means no accidental dependencies on platform-specific features, no gateway abstractions that subtly encourage vendor adoption, and no marketing surface embedded in your technical stack.\n\n### Code Mode\n\nCode Mode lets the model write TypeScript that calls your tools inside a sandbox. One `execute_typescript` call can loop, branch, and `Promise.all` instead of one tool per turn.\n\nTanStack AI ships six isolate drivers behind one `IsolateDriver` interface:\n\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-node`** - Node.js sandbox via `isolated-vm`\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-quickjs`** - QuickJS WASM (browsers and edge)\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-quickjs-bun`** - Native QuickJS on Bun via `bun:ffi`\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-cloudflare`** - Cloudflare Workers\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-daytona`** - Remote Daytona sandbox\n- **`@tanstack/ai-isolate-e2b`** - Remote E2B sandbox\n\nSwap the driver without changing application code. A companion `@tanstack/ai-code-mode-snippets` package gives the model a persistent snippet library. The model can save working TypeScript snippets, list them, and reuse them across sessions. Trust strategies control what gets promoted to a first-class tool.\n\nVercel AI SDK now ships experimental `@ai-sdk/code-mode`. It runs QuickJS only, needs Node 22 or newer, and does not run in the browser or on the edge. Nested tool approvals are rejected. Provider-hosted code execution (Anthropic, xAI, OpenAI) is still a separate path. None of those give the model a persistent, provider-agnostic skill library it builds itself. See [Code Mode](../code-mode/code-mode).\n\n### Coding Agent Sandboxes\n\nSeparately from the JS isolates above, TanStack AI can put a full coding-agent CLI inside an isolated sandbox with a real filesystem, a shell, and a cloned repo. The agent can be Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, or any ACP-compliant agent. Its work streams back through `chat()` like any other run.\n\nA sandboxed run composes three swappable pieces:\n\n- a **provider** (where it runs)\n- a **workspace** (what the agent sees)\n- a **harness adapter** (which agent runs)\n\nThe sandbox is a `chat()` middleware, so the agent's edits and commands arrive as the same AG-UI stream every `useChat` UI already renders.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { grokBuildText } from '@tanstack/ai-grok-build'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo, withSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { messages, threadId } from './chat-context'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'TanStack/ai' }),\n    packageManager: 'pnpm',\n  }),\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  threadId,\n  adapter: grokBuildText('grok-build'),\n  messages,\n  middleware: [withSandbox(sandbox)],\n})\n```\n\nTwo axes are open where the AI SDK's is narrower:\n\n- **Any agent, not a fixed list.** Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode ship as first-party harness packages, and `acpCompatible` (from `@tanstack/ai-acp`) turns *any* [Agent Client Protocol](https://agentclientprotocol.com) agent (`pi`, `gemini --acp`, and [dozens of others](https://agentclientprotocol.com/get-started/agents)) into a harness by describing how to launch it. Adding an agent does not require a dedicated adapter to exist.\n- **Any sandbox, not one cloud.** The same run executes on `localProcessSandbox` (host dev loop), `dockerSandbox` (container), `sbxSandbox` (Docker Sandboxes microVM), Daytona, Vercel Sandbox, Sprites, or Cloudflare. Swap the provider without touching the harness or workspace. Providers declare their `capabilities()` (`fs`, `exec`, `ports`, `snapshots`, `fork`, `durableFilesystem`, and more) so code degrades across them.\n\nVercel AI SDK 7 added an experimental `HarnessAgent` API for the same idea: run a coding-agent harness in a sandbox and return AI SDK `generate()` / `stream()` results. It ships adapters for Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, Deep Agents, and Pi. The documented path runs them in Vercel Sandbox, with long work on the Workflow SDK. There is no generic ACP escape hatch (each supported harness is its own package), and sandbox support centers on Vercel's own microVM rather than a provider-swappable contract. See [Sandbox providers](../sandbox/providers) and [Harnesses](../sandbox/harnesses).\n\n### Media Generation\n\nTanStack AI provides stable, dedicated APIs for every media generation activity - image, video, speech, transcription, and summarization. Each is a separate, tree-shakeable function with its own adapter per provider.\n\nVercel AI SDK has added several of these capabilities. As of v6, `generateImage()` is stable; video generation is still experimental (`experimental_generateVideo()`); and `generateSpeech()` / `transcribe()` are exported without the `experimental_` prefix but are still documented as experimental features. TanStack AI's media APIs are stable across the board and go further in several areas:\n\n**Image generation** - `generateImage()` with per-model type safety. TypeScript knows that `gpt-image-2` and `dall-e-3` expose different size constraints. Five providers ship adapters: OpenAI (GPT Image, DALL-E), Gemini (Imagen), Grok, OpenRouter, and fal.ai (600+ community models including Flux, SDXL, and more).\n\n```ts\nimport { generateImage } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiImage } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst result = await generateImage({\n  adapter: openaiImage('gpt-image-2'),\n  prompt: 'A sunset over mountains',\n  size: '1536x1024',\n  numberOfImages: 1,\n})\n```\n\n**Video generation** - `generateVideo()` handles the full async job lifecycle automatically. Video generation APIs are inherently asynchronous - you submit a job, poll for status, and eventually get a result. TanStack AI manages this entire lifecycle with configurable polling intervals and timeouts, streaming status updates back to the client.\n\n```ts\nimport { generateVideo } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiVideo } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst stream = generateVideo({\n  adapter: openaiVideo('sora-2'),\n  prompt: 'A cat playing piano',\n  size: '1280x720',\n  duration: 8,\n  stream: true,          // Stream job lifecycle events\n  pollingInterval: 2000, // Poll every 2 seconds\n})\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  // Receive: job created → status updates → final video URL\n}\n```\n\nVercel AI SDK's `experimental_generateVideo()` returns the video directly without exposing the job lifecycle or streaming status updates.\n\n**Text-to-speech** - `generateSpeech()` supports 6 audio output formats (mp3, opus, aac, flac, wav, pcm), speed control (0.25x to 4x), and five providers: OpenAI (11 voices), Gemini (30+ voices with language hints), Grok, ElevenLabs, and fal.ai.\n\n```ts\nimport { generateSpeech } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiSpeech } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst result = await generateSpeech({\n  adapter: openaiSpeech('tts-1-hd'),\n  text: 'Hello, world!',\n  voice: 'nova',\n  format: 'opus',\n  speed: 1.2,\n})\n```\n\n**Transcription** - `generateTranscription()` supports common output formats (json, text, srt, verbose_json, vtt), word-level timestamps with confidence scores, and four providers (OpenAI, Grok, ElevenLabs, fal.ai), with speaker diarization via OpenAI's `gpt-4o-transcribe-diarize` model.\n\n```ts\nimport { generateTranscription } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiTranscription } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { audioFile } from './audio'\n\nconst result = await generateTranscription({\n  adapter: openaiTranscription('gpt-4o-transcribe'),\n  audio: audioFile,\n  responseFormat: 'verbose_json', // Includes word-level timestamps\n})\n\n// result.words → [{ word: 'Hello', start: 0.0, end: 0.42 }, ...]\n```\n\n**Audio & music generation** - `generateAudio()` generates music and sound effects across Gemini (Lyria), ElevenLabs (music + sound effects), and fal.ai. Vercel AI SDK has no equivalent.\n\n**Summarization** - `summarize()` is a dedicated activity with style control (`bullet-points`, `paragraph`, `concise`), focus topics, and streaming support. Vercel AI SDK has no equivalent - summarization requires calling `generateText()` with a prompt.\n\n**Realtime voice** - `realtimeToken()` enables bidirectional audio streaming with Voice Activity Detection modes (server, semantic, manual), tool calling during voice sessions, and simultaneous audio + text output. Three providers ship realtime adapters: OpenAI (Realtime API), Grok, and ElevenLabs. Vercel AI SDK now ships experimental `experimental_useRealtime` for OpenAI, Google, and xAI (plus AI Gateway). TanStack AI's realtime adapters are stable and include ElevenLabs.\n\nAll media activities follow the same adapter pattern as chat - tree-shakeable imports, per-model type safety, and streaming support. If your app only uses chat, none of this media code enters your bundle.\n\n### Native AG-UI Protocol\n\nThe events TanStack AI streams between server and client are [AG-UI](https://docs.ag-ui.com/) events (`RUN_STARTED`, `TEXT_MESSAGE_*`, `TOOL_CALL_*`, `RUN_FINISHED`), imported directly from `@ag-ui/core` - not a bespoke format with an AG-UI export bolted on. Anything that speaks AG-UI can sit on either side of the wire: AG-UI-compliant agent frameworks behind a TanStack AI frontend, or a TanStack AI client in front of an agent server written in another language entirely.\n\nVercel AI SDK streams its own proprietary UI Message Stream protocol. AG-UI interop requires an external translation layer (`@ag-ui/vercel-ai-sdk`, built and maintained by the AG-UI project), and native support remains an open feature request on the AI SDK repo.\n\n### Hooks for Every Activity\n\nChat isn't the only activity with a hook. Every activity ships one - `useGeneration` (streaming structured output), `useGenerateImage`, `useGenerateAudio`, `useGenerateSpeech`, `useTranscription`, `useSummarize`, `useGenerateVideo`, and `useRealtimeChat` - with the same connection-adapter wiring and devtools integration as `useChat`, across React, Solid, Vue, Svelte, Preact, and Angular.\n\nVercel AI SDK's UI layer has three hooks: `useChat`, `useCompletion`, and `useObject`. Its media functions (`generateImage()`, `experimental_generateVideo()`, speech, transcription) are server-side only - surfacing them in a UI means hand-rolling your own routes and client state.\n\n### Multi-Turn Structured Output\n\nTanStack AI preserves structured output in conversation history instead of leaving it only on a call result. Providers may produce it in the agent loop or through separate finalization; both paths create a typed `StructuredOutputPart`, streamed as a `partial` and completed as a `final`, with the schema generic threading all the way down to `messages[i].parts[j].data`.\n\nVercel AI SDK's structured output (`generateObject` / `streamObject` / `Output`) is per-call: the typed object lives on the call result, the message-part union has no structured-output type, and combining `useChat` with typed structured output means manually parsing model text into custom data parts.\n\n### Debug Logging\n\nSet `debug: true` on any activity and the pipeline prints itself: raw provider chunks, post-middleware output, middleware hook inputs and outputs, tool execution, agent-loop iterations, config transforms, and request metadata - each category individually toggleable, with a pluggable `logger` for structured output. Vercel AI SDK's built-in logging covers provider warnings; richer observability goes through experimental telemetry hooks or the dev-only DevTools recorder rather than a debug log you can flip on anywhere.\n\n### Community Adapter Ecosystem\n\nTanStack AI publishes an open adapter specification. Official LLM adapters:\n\n- OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Vertex, Grok, Groq, OpenRouter, Ollama\n- Bedrock, BytePlus, Mistral, Cohere, ElevenLabs, fal\n- Vercel AI Gateway, and any OpenAI-compatible endpoint via `openaiCompatible`\n\nHarness adapters: Grok Build, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, and any ACP agent via `acpCompatible`. The community has already built adapters for Decart, Cencori, Cloudflare, Soniox, and Mynth, with a [guide for building your own](../community-adapters/guide).\n\n## Where Vercel AI SDK Excels\n\n**Provider breadth.** Vercel AI SDK ships ~38 first-party, individually typed provider packages, plus a large community list. If you want a specific provider as a dedicated, maintained package without writing an adapter, their coverage is still broader today. Raw model *count* is not the differentiator: TanStack AI's OpenRouter adapter reaches OpenRouter's full catalog, the first-party Vercel Gateway adapter routes with one key, and `openaiCompatible` connects to any OpenAI-compatible endpoint.\n\n**Solid vs Angular.** Both SDKs now ship official Angular. Solid still cuts the other way: AI SDK's Solid package is community-maintained and pinned to an older SDK major, while TanStack AI ships an official, current Solid integration.\n\n**Agent abstraction.** Vercel AI SDK ships a dedicated `Agent` abstraction (the `ToolLoopAgent` class) that packages a model, tools, instructions, and loop settings into a reusable object with `.generate()` and `.stream()` methods, plus `InferAgentUIMessage` for end-to-end type safety. TanStack AI composes these pieces per call rather than offering a single agent class.\n\n**AI Gateway.** Vercel's optional AI Gateway adds centralized provider management: failover routing, caching, and a single key across providers, integrated with the Vercel platform (and used by default when no provider is configured). TanStack AI ships no gateway of its own. For the same centralized routing, use the first-class [OpenRouter](../adapters/openrouter) adapter or the [Vercel AI Gateway](../adapters/vercel-gateway) adapter. Neither attaches a platform layer to the rest of your stack.\n\n**React Server Components.** Vercel AI SDK has an RSC integration via `@ai-sdk/rsc` (`AIState`, `StreamableValue`, `streamUI`). Note that Vercel documents this as experimental and recommends AI SDK UI for production - so it's an option for Next.js RSC apps rather than the primary path.\n\n## Side-by-Side: Key Differences\n\n### Tool Definition\n\n**TanStack AI** - Isomorphic definitions with separate runtime implementations:\n\n```ts\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { weatherApi } from './weather'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Get current weather for a location',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temp: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n})\n\n// Server implementation\nconst getWeatherServer = getWeather.server(async ({ city }) => {\n  const data = await weatherApi.get(city)\n  return { temp: data.temperature, condition: data.condition }\n})\n\n// Client implementation\nconst getWeatherClient = getWeather.client(async ({ city }) => {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/weather?city=${city}`)\n  return res.json()\n})\n```\n\n**Vercel AI SDK** - Tool objects via the `tool()` helper:\n\n```ts\nimport { generateText, tool } from 'ai'\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\nimport { weatherApi } from './weather'\n\nconst result = await generateText({\n  model: openai('gpt-5.5'),\n  tools: {\n    getWeather: tool({\n      description: 'Get current weather for a location',\n      inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n      execute: async ({ city }) => {\n        const data = await weatherApi.get(city)\n        return { temp: data.temperature, condition: data.condition }\n      },\n    }),\n  },\n  prompt: \"What's the weather in Tokyo?\",\n})\n```\n\nThe TanStack approach separates the tool contract from its implementation, making tools reusable across server and client contexts.\n\n### Persistence\n\n**TanStack AI** - Middleware writes the transcript. The client reloads it.\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  threadId: 'support-chat',\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n  persistence: true,\n})\n```\n\n**Vercel AI SDK** - You own `saveChat` / `loadChat` and call them from `onEnd`:\n\n```ts\nimport { convertToModelMessages, createUIMessageStreamResponse, streamText, toUIMessageStream } from 'ai'\nimport { loadChat, saveChat } from './chat-store'\n\nexport async function POST(req: Request) {\n  const { message, id } = await req.json()\n  const messages = [...(await loadChat(id)), message]\n\n  const result = streamText({\n    model: 'openai/gpt-5.5',\n    messages: await convertToModelMessages(messages),\n  })\n\n  return createUIMessageStreamResponse({\n    stream: toUIMessageStream({\n      stream: result.stream,\n      originalMessages: messages,\n      onEnd: ({ messages: next }) => {\n        saveChat({ chatId: id, messages: next })\n      },\n    }),\n  })\n}\n```\n\nTanStack AI also persists run status and pending approvals, and it can restore a long media generation into the same hook fields. Vercel AI SDK leaves that wiring to you. Resume of a live stream is a separate layer on both sides: TanStack AI uses `StreamDurability`, Vercel AI SDK uses Redis plus `resumable-stream`.\n\n### Agent Loop Control\n\n**TanStack AI** - Composable strategies:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, combineStrategies, maxIterations, untilFinishReason } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\nimport { estimatedTokens } from './cost'\n\nconst messages = [{ role: 'user' as const, content: 'Help me plan a trip.' }]\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages,\n  tools,\n  agentLoopStrategy: combineStrategies([\n    maxIterations(10),\n    untilFinishReason(['stop']),\n    ({ iterationCount }) => estimatedTokens(iterationCount) < 50_000,\n  ]),\n})\n```\n\n**Vercel AI SDK** - `stopWhen` conditions (v5+):\n\n```ts\nimport { generateText, stepCountIs } from 'ai'\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'\nimport { tools } from './tools'\n\nconst result = await generateText({\n  model: openai('gpt-5.5'),\n  tools,\n  stopWhen: stepCountIs(10), // also: hasToolCall('name'), or a custom function\n  prompt: 'Help me plan a trip.',\n})\n```\n\nBoth let you compose multiple stopping conditions - `stopWhen` accepts an array of conditions including custom functions, and v6 adds a reusable `Agent` class. The remaining nuance is ergonomic: TanStack AI's strategies are plain `(state) => boolean` predicates combined with `combineStrategies`, so token budgets and custom business logic are first-class without reaching for a built-in condition.\n\n### Tree-Shaking\n\n**TanStack AI** - Separate adapters per activity:\n\n```ts\n// Only bundles chat + OpenAI text adapter\nimport { chat } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n```\n\n**Vercel AI SDK** - Single provider import:\n\n```ts\n// Provider package includes all model types\nimport { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai'\n```\n\nIn TanStack AI, each activity (chat, image, speech, video, transcription, summarization) is a separate adapter function. You import `openaiText` for chat and `openaiImage` for image generation - they're independent modules. Vercel AI SDK's provider packages are more monolithic.\n\n## When to Choose TanStack AI\n\n- **Bundle size matters** - Tree-shakeable adapters per activity mean smaller bundles\n- **AG-UI native** - The wire protocol is AG-UI end to end; interoperate with the agent-UI ecosystem and non-TypeScript agent servers without a translation layer\n- **Solid, Preact, Angular, or React Native** - One headless core covers React, Solid, Vue, Svelte, Preact, Angular, and React Native (via XHR adapters), all officially maintained\n- **Hooks beyond chat** - `useGeneration`, `useGenerateImage`, `useSummarize`, and the rest of the generation hook family across every supported framework\n- **Isomorphic tools** - Define a tool once and derive `.server()` / `.client()` implementations from one contract\n- **App-level middleware** - Lifecycle hooks for chunks, tool calls, usage, and errors - not just model wrapping\n- **Chat and generation persistence** - `withPersistence` plus `reconstructChat`, or a browser store; long media runs restore through `withGenerationPersistence`\n- **Resumable streams** - A durability adapter on the response; `useChat` reconnects without Redis\n- **Durable sandbox runs** - Detach, journal, takeover, and reaper so a coding agent survives a closed tab\n- **Realtime voice** - Stable bidirectional audio across OpenAI, Grok, and ElevenLabs\n- **No vendor association** - Pure library with no platform layer\n- **Per-model type safety** - TypeScript narrows options per model, not per provider\n- **Code Mode** - Five isolate drivers (Node, QuickJS WASM, QuickJS Bun, Cloudflare, Daytona) plus an LLM-writable skill library\n- **Coding agent sandboxes** - Run Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build, OpenCode, or any ACP agent in a swappable sandbox (local, Docker, Docker Sandboxes, Daytona, Vercel, Sprites, Cloudflare), streamed through `chat()`\n- **Flexible transport** - SSE, HTTP streams, XHR, RPC, direct iterables, or custom adapters\n- **MCP, two ways** - A standalone host-side client (`@tanstack/ai-mcp`) with pools, codegen, and managed `chat()` lifecycle, plus a provider-routed `mcpTool()`\n\n## When to Choose Vercel AI SDK\n\n- **Need a first-party package for a specific provider** - ~38 dedicated, individually typed provider packages today (TanStack reaches comparable model breadth via OpenRouter, Vercel Gateway, and `openaiCompatible`)\n- **Agent abstraction** - A reusable `Agent` (`ToolLoopAgent`) class with end-to-end UI message types\n- **Vercel platform** - AI Gateway, observability, and deployment optimization\n- **React Server Components** - RSC primitives via `@ai-sdk/rsc` (experimental; AI SDK UI is the recommended production path)\n- **Workflow-backed long runs on Vercel** - experimental `HarnessAgent` plus the Workflow SDK, if your stack is already that platform\n\n## Getting Started\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-react @tanstack/ai-openai\nvue: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-vue @tanstack/ai-openai\nsolid: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-solid @tanstack/ai-openai\nsvelte: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-svelte @tanstack/ai-openai\npreact: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-preact @tanstack/ai-openai\nangular: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-angular @tanstack/ai-openai\nvanilla: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-client @tanstack/ai-openai\noctane: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-octane @tanstack/ai-openai octane\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\nSee the [Quick Start Guide](../getting-started/quick-start) to build your first chat application, or explore the [full documentation](../getting-started/overview).\n"}
